        "id": "00-002",
        "number": 2,
        "title": "Static Asset URL Import",
        "local_chapter_file": "002-static-asset-url-import.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "An import that hands back an address, not a package",
          "Dev vs. build: what the string actually is",
          "Minimal example",
          "Predict what changes",
          "Common mistakes",
          "Why this matters"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 1132,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "You already import values from other modules: a function, a constant, a component. `import logoUrl from \"./logo.png\"` looks the same, but a PNG has no exports. There's no function or object living inside it for a bundler to hand you.  What Vite hands you instead is a string, the URL where that file will be reachable once it's served. Think of it like a coat check ticket rather than the coat itself: you don't carry the image's bytes around in your JavaScript, you carry a short string that tells the browser where to go fetch them when it needs to paint an `<img>` or a CSS background.",
          "mental_model": "While the dev server is running, Vite doesn't touch the file at all. It just serves `src/logo.png` from disk at the path `/src/logo.png`, the same way it would serve any other file under `src/`. The import resolves to that path because that's genuinely where the file lives, reachable over HTTP.  `bun run build` is different. Vite reads every asset reachable from your module graph, copies each one into `dist/assets/`, and renames it with a content hash baked into the filename: `logo.2d8efhg.png`. The import now resolves to that hashed path instead of the original name.",
          "code_example": "import logoUrl from \"./logo.png\";\n\nexport function Header() {\n  return (\n    <header>\n      <img src={logoUrl} alt=\"Acme company logo\" width={32} height={32} />\n      <h1>Acme</h1>\n    </header>\n  );\n}",
          "pitfalls": "**Forgetting the type declarations that make the import legal at all.** TypeScript has no built-in idea that `.png` files can be imported as strings. Without a reference to Vite's client types, this is the exact error:  The fix is a `vite-env.d.ts` file at your project root containing:  `create-vite` scaffolds this file for you automatically; if you ever hand-roll a project or hit this error in a file outside a scaffolded app, add the reference yourself.  **Treating the imported value as the file, not its address.** `logoUrl` is a `string`. Calling a file-like method on it doesn't compile:  If you need the actual bytes in JavaScript (to hash them yourself, say, or feed them to a canvas), `fetch(logoUrl)` and read the response. The import only ever gives you where to find the file, never its contents."
        },
