        "id": "00-003",
        "number": 3,
        "title": "Compile-Time Global Constants",
        "local_chapter_file": "003-compile-time-global-constants.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "A word that gets erased before your code ever runs",
          "The concept: `define`",
          "Minimal example",
          "Predict the build",
          "Common mistakes",
          "Why this matters"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 470,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "A mail-merge letter has a placeholder in it: \"Dear {{name}},\" Before a single copy gets printed, a program scans the template and swaps `{{name}}` for \"Ada\" everywhere it appears. The printed letter never contains the placeholder text, only the name. Nobody reading the finished letter could tell a placeholder was ever there, and nothing looks the value up while the letter is being read.  Vite's `define` option runs the same trick on your source code. You write an identifier like `__APP_VERSION__` in a component. Before the browser (or the production bundle) ever sees that file, Vite deletes the identifier and pastes in a literal value instead. There's no variable called `__APP_VERSION__` sitting in memory at runtime holding a string. The identifier is gone, replaced by the value itself, the moment the file is compiled.",
          "mental_model": "Production error trackers like Sentry tag every report with a release version, usually a `define`d constant baked in at build time rather than read from a file at runtime, so a crash report always points at the exact code that produced it. Feature flags that only exist for a staging build get `define`d to `false` in production, which does more than hide a UI element: an `if (__IS_STAGING__) { ... }` branch guarded by a constant that's always `false` is dead code the production bundler can delete entirely, the same tree-shaking mechanism a later chapter covers in depth. A runtime `if (checkFlag())` check can't be eliminated that way, because the bundler has no way to know, at build time, what the function will return.  The bug class this prevents: a value that should never change after the app ships (a version string, a compiled-in feature flag, a build timestamp) living as a runtime lookup instead, with all the extra code, and all the ways that lookup can silently return something unexpected, that a lookup implies.",
          "code_example": "// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from \"vite\";\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  define: {\n    __APP_VERSION__: JSON.stringify(\"1.4.0\"),\n  },\n});",
          "pitfalls": "**Passing a raw string instead of a JSON-stringified one.**  Since `Footer.tsx` uses this as `build {__APP_VERSION__}`, the substitution produces `build {1.4.0}`, which isn't valid JavaScript (a number literal can't have two decimal points). `bun run build` fails with:  The fix is `JSON.stringify(\"1.4.0\")`, so the value handed to `define` is the six characters `\"1.4.0\"`, quotes included: valid JSON, and it splices in as a proper string literal rather than bare, syntactically broken source text.  **Using a relative path in `resolve.alias`.**  Building this produces:"
        },
