        "id": "00-005",
        "number": 5,
        "title": "Vite Modern Web Constraints",
        "local_chapter_file": "005-vite-modern-web-constraints.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "A backstage door that doesn't open onto the stage",
          "The concept: Vite's modern-web bias",
          "Minimal example",
          "Predict the build",
          "Common mistakes",
          "Why this matters"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 387,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "A theater's backstage crew can do things the actors on stage never could: hammer together a set piece, splice a frayed extension cord, look up a prop's shipping manifest. None of that machinery travels onto the stage once the curtain goes up. An actor who suddenly needs a hammer mid-scene doesn't get one. The workshop and the stage are two different places with two different rules, and the curtain is the boundary between them.  `vite.config.ts`, and everything a Vite plugin does inside it, is the workshop: it runs in Node, before your app ever reaches a browser, so it can import anything Node offers (`fs`, `path`, `child_process`, any npm package built for a server). Your components live somewhere else entirely: a browser tab, alive only after the curtain (an actual `<script type=\"module\">` tag) goes up. Reach for a Node built-in from inside a component and you're asking an actor to produce a backstage hammer mid-scene. It was never shipped there.",
          "mental_model": "Older bundlers earned their large default bundle sizes partly by trying to make every package \"just work\" in a browser, Node built-ins included, whether or not the code path that needed them ever actually ran client-side. Vite's refusal to do that is why a fresh Vite + React project ships a noticeably smaller bundle than the same dependencies run through a legacy Webpack config with its default Node polyfills turned on: nothing gets shimmed in that your code doesn't genuinely use, and anything that tries to reach a Node built-in fails immediately during development, not weeks later when a user's bundle silently misbehaves in production.  The same bias is why Next.js, Remix, and most modern React meta-frameworks draw the identical line between \"server code\" and \"client code\" that this chapter draws between Node and the browser. The specific keyword differs (`\"use server\"` and `\"use client\"` directives instead of the plain fact of which file imports what), but the underlying rule, that browser-bound code can't silently reach into server-only primitives, is the same one Vite enforces here first.",
          "code_example": "// src/sorter.ts\nself.onmessage = (event: MessageEvent<number[]>) => {\n  const sorted = [...event.data].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  self.postMessage(sorted);\n};",
          "pitfalls": "**Importing a Node built-in from a component, expecting it to work because it compiles.** `@types/node` is often present in a project (for `vite.config.ts` itself), so `import { readFileSync } from \"node:fs\"` inside a `.tsx` file type-checks cleanly under `bunx tsc --noEmit`. The failure only shows up at runtime, in the browser console, and it's the exact text shown above:  The fix is never \"polyfill it.\" Move the actual file or filesystem work into a Vite plugin (Node-side, config-time) or a small backend endpoint the component calls with `fetch`, the same way any browser-only code has to reach server-only data.  **Storing the worker's `new URL(...)` in a variable before passing it to `new Worker`.**  Vite's worker detection only fires when the `new URL(...)` expression sits directly inside the `new Worker(...)` call. Written this way, `sorter.ts` never gets its own bundled entry chunk in production. The site's SPA fallback middleware (`appType: \"spa\"`, covered in `compile-time-global-constants.md`) serves `index.html` for the now-missing path instead, and the browser throws the real, standard error for a module script that resolved to the wrong content type:  The fix is inlining the `new URL(...)` call directly as the first argument to `new Worker(...)`, exactly as in the working example above."
        },
