        "id": "00-007",
        "number": 7,
        "title": "Library Mode (`build.lib`)",
        "local_chapter_file": "007-library-mode-build-lib.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Shipping a part, not deploying a page",
          "The concept: `build.lib`",
          "Predict the build: one `entry` string vs. one `entry` object",
          "Combining configs without repeating them: `mergeConfig` and `resolveConfig`",
          "Vite 8: Rolldown replaces esbuild and Rollup",
          "Vite as a foundation other tools build on"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 694,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Every Vite config so far in this course has answered one question: how does this one app get from source to a browser at one URL. `environment.md`'s `dist/` folder, `vite-root-base-mode-and-input-options.md`'s `base` and `input`, `compile-time-global-constants.md`'s dependency pre-bundling. All of it assumes a single destination: your own deployed site, built once, served from one place.  Acme's design system team has a different problem. `packages/acme-ui-kit` isn't a page. It's a `<Button>`, a `<Dialog>`, a handful of hooks, imported by three other teams' apps, each on its own React version, its own bundler, its own deploy schedule. Nobody at Acme controls what happens after `npm install acme-ui-kit`. The output has to be a set of files a stranger's toolchain can consume, not a folder a stranger's browser can visit.",
          "mental_model": "Every other Vite config decision in this course optimizes one app's own build. Library mode is the point where Acme's Vite config stops being about Acme's own deploy and starts being a contract with code the team doesn't control: what gets bundled, what stays external, what filename a `package.json` can rely on existing. Getting `external` wrong doesn't fail a test locally, it ships a duplicate React into every app that depends on the library, the exact class of bug that only surfaces as a confusing runtime warning in someone else's project. The Vite 8 migration section matters for a similar reason: none of this course's own configs change shape because of it, but the moment a teammate pastes a config snippet from an older tutorial, knowing `build.rollupOptions` still works (converted automatically) versus knowing it's the name to actively avoid in new code is the difference between quietly accumulating deprecated options and writing today's idiomatic config from the start.",
          "code_example": "interface LibraryOptions {\n  entry: string | string[] | Record<string, string>;\n  name?: string; // required for the umd/iife formats\n  fileName?: string | ((format: string, entryName: string) => string);\n  formats?: (\"es\" | \"cjs\" | \"umd\" | \"iife\")[];\n  cssFileName?: string;\n}",
          "pitfalls": "**Forgetting `rolldownOptions.external` on a library build.** The build still succeeds, `dist/acme-ui-kit.js` still works. The problem shows up one install later, in a consuming app: two copies of React exist in the page (the library's bundled one, the app's own), React throws its \"Invalid hook call\" warning, or components from the library silently fail to share context with the rest of the app, because `useContext` in the library's bundled React instance can never see a `Provider` rendered by the app's own React instance.  **Copying a `package.json` `exports` map from a single-entry build onto a multi-entry one (or the reverse).** As the predict-the-build exercise above showed, single-entry defaults to `[\"es\", \"umd\"]`, multi-entry defaults to `[\"es\", \"cjs\"]`. Pointing `exports[\".\"].require` at `acme-ui-kit.umd.cjs` when the actual build only produced `acme-ui-kit.cjs` (or vice versa) compiles the `package.json` fine (it's JSON, nothing type-checks it against the real `dist/` contents) and only breaks the moment `require(\"acme-ui-kit\")` runs against a file that was never built:  **Calling `import.meta.hot.accept` without the exact call shape Vite expects.** The HMR API's static analysis requires the call to appear literally as `import.meta.hot.accept(` in the source, whitespace-sensitive. Assigning `import.meta.hot` to a variable first (`const hot = import.meta.hot; hot.accept(...)`) compiles and runs, but Vite's static scan never sees an `accept(` call directly on `import.meta.hot`, so the module never becomes an HMR boundary, and every edit to it falls through to a full page reload instead.  **Reaching for `build.rollupOptions` in a Vite 8 project because that's what older tutorials show.** It still works, the compatibility layer converts it to `build.rolldownOptions` automatically. The option is deprecated and scheduled for removal, and a config that keeps using the old name gets no benefit from the newer `rolldownOptions`-only features (Rolldown's `codeSplitting"
        },
