              "pitfalls": "**Importing a virtual module from a `.ts`/`.tsx` file with no ambient declaration.** `tsc --noEmit` doesn't run Vite's plugins, so it has no `resolveId` or `load` to ask. It reports exactly the error you'd get for any genuinely missing module:  The fix is the `declare module \"virtual:build-info\" { ... }` block from the combined example above, usually kept in one shared `vite-env.d.ts` alongside the `/// <reference types=\"vite/client\" />` line `vite-env-variables-and-modes-import-meta-env.md` already introduced.  **A `load` filter that doesn't match what `resolveId` actually returned.** Copy-pasting a virtual-module plugin and renaming the id in `resolveId` but missing one spot in `load`'s filter leaves `load` unable to recognize the id `resolveId` just resolved. No plugin's `load` claims it, so Vite falls back to its default file-loading behavior, which tries to read the id as a path. Since that id still contains the literal NUL byte, Node's own `fs` layer rejects it before a file system call ever happens:  **Expecting `this.emitFile()` (or any other output-generation hook) called from a virtual module's `load` to behave the same in dev as in a build.** Output-generation hooks, `generateBundle` and friends, only run during `vite build` (aside from `closeBundle`, which runs in both). A plugin that tries to emit a companion asset file from inside `load` works perfectly under `bun run build` and silently emits nothing under `bun run dev`, since dev mode never reaches that phase of the pipeline at all.  **Adding a virtual-module plugin to the top-level `plugins` array and assuming a Web Worker sees it in production too.** `config.plugins` reaches worker code during development, but a production worker build only sees plugins listed in `worker.plugins`. `bun run dev` runs clean; `bun run build` fails to resolve the same import inside the worker's bundle:"
