        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "`virtual-modules-virtual-convention.md` ended with a working SSR preview server: `vite.middlewares` mounted on a plain `node:http` server, one request handler reading `index.html`, calling `vite.transformIndexHtml`, then `vite.ssrLoadModule('/src/entry-server.tsx')` to render. That setup has exactly two things going on: a client bundle (the browser tab) and one \"ssr\" bundle (the render call, running in Node). Vite's older APIs treat that second thing as a single boolean, `ssr: true`, threaded through hooks and options wherever it matters.  A single breaker switch is fine for a house with one circuit beyond the main one. It stops being fine the day you need a second: an edge runtime (a Cloudflare Worker rendering a subset of routes), a separate React Server Components graph, a worker-thread pool. `ssr: true` can only ever answer \"is this the one other thing, or not.\" It has no way to say *which* other thing, once there's more than one.",
          "mental_model": "Every framework that embeds Vite in a custom dev server, an SSR meta- framework, a component-explorer tool, a test runner's browser mode, goes through exactly the `middlewareMode` plus `appType: \"custom\"` combination this chapter opened with. Reading one of those tools' source for the first time is a lot less mysterious once `vite.middlewares`, `appType`, and `transformIndexHtml` read as \"the same three pieces this course already built a small version of,\" rather than framework-specific magic.  The Environment API matters for the same reason a bit further out: a project that later adds React Server Components (this course's own RSC chapters return to this) typically needs a distinct environment for the RSC module graph, separate from `client` and `ssr`, each with its own resolve conditions. Recognizing `this.environment.name`, `applyToEnvironment`, and `server.environments` on sight is what turns \"this plugin's config looks completely different from a normal Vite plugin\" into \"this is the same panel of named environments, with a third circuit added.\"",
          "code_example": "// scripts/ssr-preview-server.ts\nimport { resolve } from \"node:path\";\nimport { readFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\nimport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\nimport { createServer as createHttpServer } from \"node:http\";\nimport { createServer } from \"vite\";\n\nconst projectRoot = fileURLToPath(new URL(\"..\", import.meta.url));\n\nconst vite = await createServer({\n  configFile: false,\n  root: projectRoot,\n  appType: \"custom\",\n  server: { middlewareMode: true },\n});\n\nvite.middlewares.use(async (req, res, next) => {\n  const url = req.originalUrl ?? req.url ?? \"/\";\n\n  try {\n    const templatePath = resolve(projectRoot, \"index.html\");\n    let template = readFileSync(templatePath, \"utf-8\");\n    template = await vite.transformIndexHtml(url, template);\n\n    const ssrEnvironment = vite.environments.ssr;\n    const { render } = await ssrEnvironment.runner.import(\"/src/entry-server.tsx\");\n    const appHtml = (await render(url)) as string;\n\n    const html = template.replace(\"<!--ssr-outlet-->\", () => appHtml);\n    res.statusCode = 200;\n    res.setHeader(\"Content-Type\", \"text/html\");\n    res.end(html);\n  } catch (error) {\n    vite.ssrFixStacktrace(error as Error);\n    next(error);\n  }\n});\n\nconst httpServer = createHttpServer(vite.middlewares);\nhttpServer.listen(4322, () => {\n  console.log(\"SSR preview server ready at http://localhost:4322\");\n});",
          "pitfalls": "**Setting `middlewareMode: true` and expecting HTML routing to already be disabled.** As above: `curl` returns the client shell, not your rendered page, and no error is thrown anywhere, which is what makes this one easy to miss in development. The fix is `appType: \"custom\"` alongside `middlewareMode: true`, not instead of it.  **Branching on `options?.ssr` once a third environment exists.** A hook written against the old two-way flag:  `options?.ssr` is falsy for `client`, and also falsy for a newly-added `edge` environment, since that flag never had a third state to represent \"edge.\" `injectBrowserGlobals` now runs for `edge` too, and the edge bundle throws the moment it actually executes in a runtime with no `window`:  Checking `this.environment.name === \"client\"` instead of `!options?.ssr` says exactly what's meant, and doesn't silently include every environment that isn't literally the old `ssr` one.  **Keying plugin state by module id alone.** Shown above in full: a shared `Map<string, number>` collapses `client` and `ssr`'s separate transform counts into one number that's wrong for both. The fix is a `Map` keyed by `this.environment.name` first, module id second (or Vite's own `perEnvironmentState()` helper), so each environment's bookkeeping stays separate from every other environment's."
        },
