        "id": "00-011",
        "number": 11,
        "title": "Vite ModuleRunner",
        "local_chapter_file": "011-vite-modulerunner.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Opening the box `ssrEnvironment.runner.import(...)` came in",
          "`ModuleRunner`: the piece that actually executes code",
          "`ModuleRunnerTransport`: the courier",
          "`ModuleEvaluator`: what actually runs the code",
          "A primer on `Promise.all`, then Vite's own JavaScript API",
          "Where this API came from: the Vite 5.1 Runtime API"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 680,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "`vite-middleware-mode.md` ended with one line quietly doing a lot of work:  That chapter treated `.runner` as a black box: call `.import(url)`, get back the same module namespace `vite.ssrLoadModule` would have handed you. This chapter opens the box. `.runner` is a `ModuleRunner`, and a `ModuleRunner` is built from three separate pieces that Vite deliberately keeps apart, because each one answers a different question.",
          "mental_model": "Almost nothing here is code you'll write in a typical app: most React projects never construct a `ModuleRunner`, a `ModuleRunnerTransport`, or a custom `ModuleEvaluator` by hand. Framework authors do, though, and the list is long, Vitest's browser mode, Storybook's Vite builder, Remix's and Next.js's own dev tooling (where it applies), any tool that says \"powered by Vite\" while running your code somewhere Vite's default Node-process shortcut doesn't reach. Recognizing `ModuleRunner`, `hotUpdate`, and `environment.moduleGraph` on sight turns reading one of those tools' source for the first time into \"this is the same three pieces this chapter named,\" instead of unfamiliar framework-specific machinery.  The SSR-facing half (externals, the manifest, `ssrFixStacktrace`) is the part of this chapter you're more likely to touch directly: any team shipping a custom SSR server, not a framework, hits the ESM-externals error at least once, and the manifest-driven preload pattern is the standard way a hand- rolled SSR setup avoids a waterfall where the browser only discovers a lazy component's file after the main bundle has already finished loading and started executing.",
          "code_example": "const { render } = await ssrEnvironment.runner.import(\"/src/entry-server.tsx\");",
          "pitfalls": "**Hand-rolling module execution instead of using `ModuleRunner`.** Reading a `.tsx` file with `fs.readFileSync` and evaluating it directly (a regex to strip `import`/`export` lines, then `new Function(...)` on what's left) skips Vite's entire transform pipeline: no JSX transform, no path alias resolution, no environment variable substitution. The moment the source contains real JSX, the raw string still contains the literal `<` from a JSX element when `new Function` tries to parse it as plain JavaScript:  `ModuleRunner.import(url)` (or `ssrEnvironment.runner.import(url)`) runs the file through the same pipeline `vite.ssrLoadModule` uses, transformed first, executed second, in that order, every time.  **Branching a `hotUpdate` (or the older `handleHotUpdate`) hook on file extension alone, ignoring which environment triggered it.** A hook that always calls `server.ws.send({ type: \"full-reload\" })` for any `.ts` file forces every connected browser tab to reload, even when the change only affects an `ssr`-only or `edge`-only module the browser never loaded in the first place. The fix is checking `this.environment.name` (in `hotUpdate`) and only forcing a reload for the environment that actually needs one, letting every other environment's own default HMR continue undisturbed.  **Assuming a full-reload's `exports` object gets refreshed in place.**  `ModuleRunner` re-executes the module on a full reload, but it overwrites the old exports object with a new one rather than mutating the one you already hold a reference to. `mod` above is that stale reference. Calling `runner.import(\"/src/config.ts\")` again after the reload (or reading from `evaluatedModules` directly) is what returns the current exports."
        },
