        "total_duration_seconds": 249,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Think of a component as a recipe card. JSX is the readable recipe notation. React reads the recipe and updates the counter when inputs change. The recipe is a description, not a list of manual DOM edits.",
          "mental_model": "This model explains malformed markup, incorrect attributes, and stale-looking output. Library code uses the same boundaries for buttons, links, fields, and layout components. Understanding that React reruns functions and reconciles returned elements helps you avoid manual DOM edits and local variables used as state. `react.dev` is the current learning and API-reference home for React.",
          "code_example": "type GreetingProps = { name: string };\n\nfunction Greeting({ name }: GreetingProps) {\n  return <p className=\"greeting\">Hello, {name}!</p>;\n}\n\nexport function App() {\n  return <Greeting name=\"Ada\" />;\n}",
          "pitfalls": "- Returning two sibling roots produces `JSX expressions must have one parent element`. Wrap them in an element or `<>...</>`. - HTML names copied unchanged produce errors such as `Property 'for' does not exist on type ...`. Use `htmlFor` and `className`. - An unclosed tag produces `JSX element 'span' has no corresponding closing tag`. Close every tag. - Calling `Greeting({ name: \"Ada\" })` bypasses the React component boundary. Use `<Greeting name=\"Ada\" />`, especially when the component later uses Hooks."
        },
