        "id": "01-007",
        "number": 7,
        "title": "Headless UI: behavior without a forced visual design",
        "local_chapter_file": "007-headless-ui-unstyled-accessible-components-for-react-vue.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Before you start",
          "1. Hook: borrow the kitchen, choose the furniture",
          "2. The concept",
          "3. Minimal example: a headless-style disclosure",
          "4. Predict the render",
          "5. Common mistakes"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 119,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Read Components and Props, JSX and Rendering, and React Major Version Policy. You need function components, JSX expressions, props, and `map()` before this chapter.",
          "mental_model": "A headless library prevents a class of bugs that only appears during real interaction: focus getting lost, `Escape` failing to close a dialog, a selected option not being announced, disabled items still receiving keyboard focus, or a menu looking open while its ARIA state says closed. These bugs survive a screenshot review.  Use a headless primitive when the interaction pattern is shared and its accessibility rules are easy to get subtly wrong. Use a native `<button>`, `<details>`, or `<select>` when it already expresses the behavior you need. Do not build a custom listbox because a styled `<select>` feels inconvenient.",
          "code_example": "import { useState } from \"react\";\n\nexport function Notice() {\n  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      <button\n        aria-expanded={open}\n        aria-controls=\"notice-panel\"\n        onClick={() => setOpen((current) => !current)}\n      >\n        Details\n      </button>\n      {open ? (\n        <p id=\"notice-panel\">Ships on Friday.</p>\n      ) : null}\n    </section>\n  );\n}",
          "pitfalls": "A menu item may look correct but be a plain `div` with a click handler. Keyboard users cannot reach it with normal menu behavior, and a screen reader may not learn which item is active. The browser does not report a TypeScript error because the markup is syntactically valid. Use the library's `Menu`, `Menu.Button`, `Menu.Items`, and `Menu.Item` roles and state management, or use native controls when the pattern is simple.  Deliberately broken JSX:  A development render can report:  Pass a function instead:  Deliberately broken TypeScript:"
        },
