        "total_duration_seconds": 599,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "The previous chapter (React Major Version Policy) covered how React's Stable version number moves: rarely at the major digit, often everywhere else. It mentioned in passing that React publishes to more than one channel and that production apps track Stable. This chapter opens that up. There are three channels, they exist for different audiences, and only one of them follows the version policy you just learned. Getting the distinction wrong is how a copy-pasted install command ends up putting `19.3.0-canary-a1b2c3d4-20260115` in your `package.json` and nobody knows why.  Alongside the channel story, this chapter gathers a handful of durable React conventions that recur in almost every component regardless of which channel you're on: how React actually puts pixels on the screen, how you render a list, how you reach a value deep in the tree without prop-drilling it, and a few of the lint rules that keep components well-formed. They're grouped here because they're the kind of foundation the hooks section ahead assumes you already have.",
          "mental_model": "The channel distinction is the difference between a boring dependency upgrade and a mystery. When your `package.json` says `^19.2.0`, the previous chapter's policy fully protects you. The moment a version string carries `-canary-` or `-experimental-`, that protection is gone by design, and the only safe move is an exact pin plus deliberate upgrades. Frameworks like Next.js make this invisible by consuming a pinned Canary for you, which is why you get new React features in Next before they hit a plain `bun add react`, without ever touching the pin yourself.  The conventions bundled into this chapter, render-and-commit, keyed lists, context, portals, clean props, `Record` lookups, the structural lint rules, aren't channel-specific. They're the substrate the hooks section assumes. Knowing that \"render\" means \"React called your function\" (not \"the DOM changed\") is what makes the coming chapters on `useState`, re-renders, and memoization comprehensible rather than magical.",
          "code_example": "# a scheduled job bumps the pin to the newest Canary, then runs the test suite\nbun add react@canary react-dom@canary",
          "pitfalls": "React keeps old APIs alive rather than forcing rewrites.  Development builds warn before they break.  Some changes never count as breaking, by policy.  Even a featureless release bumps the minor, not the patch."
        },
