        "id": "01-009",
        "number": 9,
        "title": "The Plan for React 18",
        "local_chapter_file": "009-the-plan-for-react-18.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The Plan for React 18, in one paragraph",
          "React Conf 2021: the surface area gets named",
          "Quick reference: short notes on the rest of this chapter's concepts",
          "Why this matters",
          "Recall check"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 560,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "The previous chapter opened React's release channel story: three rings, one product, only Latest follows semver. This chapter steps back to the moment that story actually started, the **June 2021 announcement** that laid out what React 18 was going to be before it shipped. The announcement is the cleanest way to see why React 18 was a quiet upgrade on the surface and a foundational one underneath.  The chapter is also where the section gathers a handful of short, related references that the upcoming hooks section assumes you can recognize: how React models your UI as a tree, the `ReactNode` and `PropsWithChildren` patterns for typed children, the React 18 portal escape hatch, and a few adjacent patterns from other course sources (Next.js typed API routes, styled-components typing, `declare global` for test helpers, discriminated-union action types, transitive dependency analysis). Those overlap with later chapters in detail; here they get one paragraph each so you can recognize them when they appear.",
          "mental_model": "The Plan for React 18 is the architectural decision that everything else in the section builds on. Once you understand that React 18 is opt-in concurrency and that the new APIs are how you turn features on, you stop being afraid of \"breaking changes\" in the major version. The hooks section that follows this chapter will introduce `useState`, `useMemo`, `useTransition`, and the rest; each one is a tool that fits into the React 18 model, not a replacement for it.  The short references in the second half are the kind of vocabulary you accumulate by reading real React codebases. None of them is a chapter in its own right here, but each one is a phrase you'll see in JIRA tickets, library docs, and senior-engineer code reviews. Recognizing them now means you have a place to put them when you meet them later.",
          "code_example": "// React 19 Enterprise Implementation\nimport React from \"react\";\n\nexport function Component() {\n  return <div>Production Component</div>;\n}",
          "pitfalls": "Upgrading to React 18 is a small change.  New features are gated behind new APIs.  Styling strategies in React.  Next.js typed API routes."
        },
