        "id": "02-003",
        "number": 3,
        "title": "preconnect and the resource-hint APIs",
        "local_chapter_file": "003-preconnect.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The five hints, cheapest to most eager",
          "Minimal example",
          "Where you can call them",
          "Packaging a hint: wrap, do not repeat",
          "Predict the render",
          "Common mistakes"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 1217,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Loading a resource from another server is a slow relay race. Before a single byte of an image or script arrives, the browser has to look up the server's IP address, open a TCP connection, and negotiate TLS. Only then can it ask for the file. If you already know the browser will need something from `cdn.example.com`, you can send it to warm up those steps early, while your component is still rendering. That is what `preconnect` and its four siblings do.  These five functions live in `react-dom`, not `react`. They are not hooks. You call them like plain functions, and each returns nothing. React deduplicates them and forwards the hint to the browser, which is free to ignore it.",
          "mental_model": "Resource hints are one of the few React features that touch the network before your data ever loads. Frameworks like Next.js call these APIs for you, so you may not write them by hand often. But knowing the ladder, connection, then bytes, then execution, tells you which lever fixes a slow third-party image, font, or embed. Wrapping the hint in a thin component keeps the CDN host in one place, and composing widgets from single-job parts keeps those components small enough to reason about. Both habits carry into every chapter after this one.",
          "code_example": "import { preconnect } from \"react-dom\";\n\nfunction Gallery() {\n  preconnect(\"https://cdn.example.com\");\n  return <img src=\"https://cdn.example.com/hero.jpg\" alt=\"Hero\" />;\n}",
          "pitfalls": "They come from `react-dom`:  Supply the resource type:  TypeScript reports that `precedence` is missing from the options for a style resource. Add it:  `preload` downloads the bytes and stops. If you preload a script and expect it to execute, nothing runs. Use `preinit` when you want the script to run or the stylesheet to apply; use `preload`/`preloadModule` only to make the later fetch instant."
        },
