              "mental_model": "Almost every interactive React component in the wild uses `useState`. A form field, a modal, a wizard's step, a toggle, a counter, a dropdown's open state, a tab panel, a theme switcher, a \"load more\" button, a to-do list. State is the boundary between \"this is a static page\" and \"this is a component you can interact with\". Getting the rules of hooks right keeps every one of those components stable across renders. Getting the immutability rules right keeps the DOM updates cheap. Getting the state shape right keeps the component's logic readable six months later.  The bigger pay-off lands when the component grows. A stateful component that calls `useEffect` to fetch data, `useMemo` to compute a derived list, `useCallback` to hand a stable callback to a memoized child, and `useId` to connect a label to an input is the canonical \"real\" React component. Every one of those hooks depends on first understanding `useState` and the rules that keep the call order stable.",
