        "id": "02-005",
        "number": 5,
        "title": "useMemo Hook",
        "local_chapter_file": "005-usememo-hook.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The concept",
          "Minimal example",
          "Why this matters in the wider hook family",
          "Predict the render",
          "Common mistakes",
          "Worked example: a slow list with a fast sibling"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 426,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Imagine you run a kitchen, and a regular customer walks in asking for the daily special. You could cook it from scratch every time they sit down, but you already have the ingredients prepped in the fridge. The smart move is to look at what changed since last time. New ingredients, new request, you cook. Nothing changed, you plate what you already made. `useMemo` is exactly that kitchen check for the result of a calculation: it caches the value, and only re-cooks when a dependency actually changes.",
          "mental_model": "`useMemo` is one piece of a larger story about values that need to persist across renders. The same chapter is a good place to meet the other \"container\" hooks because they answer different questions about persistence.  M -->|\"cached value\\nrecalculated on dep change\"| MV[Value, not reactive]   C -->|\"cached function\\nrecreated on dep change\"| CV[Stable identity]   R -->|\"stable { current }\\nmutable, non-reactive\"| RV[DOM or instance data]   I -->|\"stable string\\nunique per call site\"| IV[SSR-safe ID for labels]   X -->|\"subscribes to nearest\\nprovider value\"| XV[Cross-tree data] ```",
          "code_example": "import { useMemo, useState } from \"react\";\n\ntype Product = { id: string; name: string; price: number; rating: number };\n\nfunction ProductList({ products }: { products: Product[] }) {\n  const [sortKey, setSortKey] = useState<\"price\" | \"rating\">(\"price\");\n  const [minRating, setMinRating] = useState(0);\n\n  const visible = useMemo(() => {\n    const filtered = products.filter((p) => p.rating >= minRating);\n    return [...filtered].sort((a, b) => a[sortKey] - b[sortKey]);\n  }, [products, sortKey, minRating]);\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <label>\n        Min rating: {minRating}\n        <input\n          type=\"range\"\n          min={0}\n          max={5}\n          value={minRating}\n          onChange={(e) => setMinRating(Number(e.currentTarget.value))}\n        />\n      </label>\n      <label>\n        Sort by:{\" \"}\n        <select\n          value={sortKey}\n          onChange={(e) => setSortKey(e.currentTarget.value as \"price\" | \"rating\")}\n        >\n          <option value=\"price\">Price</option>\n          <option value=\"rating\">Rating</option>\n        </select>\n      </label>\n      <ul>\n        {visible.map((p) => (\n          <li key={p.id}>\n            {p.name} - {p.price} (rating {p.rating})\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n  );\n}",
          "pitfalls": "The lint rule `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` will warn. If you silence the warning, the cache becomes stale: when `query` changes, the previous `filtered` is still the one returned, and the UI does not update. The fix is to include `query` in the array.  `useMemo` runs during render. Network calls inside it are wrong on two counts: they violate the rule that render is pure, and they break if React decides to recompute. Move the fetch into `useEffect` and store the result in state.  `useMemo` is not free. Every call has bookkeeping cost: React stores the previous value, the previous dependencies, and runs an array compare. Wrapping a trivial addition in `useMemo` can make the component slower, not faster. The rule of thumb: memoize when the calculation is expensive, when the result is a stable reference passed to a `React.memo` child, or when the result is a stable reference used as a dependency in another hook.  React reserves the right to throw away the cache between renders. Treat `useMemo` as a performance hint, not as semantic caching. The factory must produce the same result from the same inputs. If you need a true cache (for an expensive pure function whose result you want to reuse across components), compute the value outside the component or use a `useRef` dictionary."
        },
