              "code_example": "import { useState } from \"react\";\n\ntype Item = { id: number; name: string };\n\nexport function GroceryList({ items }: { items: Item[] }) {\n  const [checked, setChecked] = useState<ReadonlySet<number>>(() => new Set());\n\n  const handleCheckChange = (id: number) => () => {\n    setChecked((prev) => {\n      const next = new Set(prev);\n      next.has(id) ? next.delete(id) : next.add(id);\n      return next;\n    });\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {items.map((item) => (\n        <li key={item.id}>\n          <label>\n            <input\n              type=\"checkbox\"\n              checked={checked.has(item.id)}\n              onChange={handleCheckChange(item.id)}\n            />\n            {item.name}\n          </label>\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  );\n}",
              "pitfalls": "- **Calling the handler instead of passing it.** `onChange={handleCheckChange(item.id)}` is correct; `onChange={handleCheckChange()}` would call the factory with `undefined` and would not adapt to each item. The factory is called *during render*, the handler is called *on click*. - **Forgetting the inner function.** `const makeHandler = (id) => doStuff(id);` returns the result of `doStuff`, not a handler. The handler generator needs `const makeHandler = (id) => () => doStuff(id);` or `const makeHandler = (id) => () => doStuff(id);`. - **Stale closures in `useEffect` dependencies.** A handler returned by the factory captures the `id` at render time. If the `id` comes from props and the parent re-renders, the factory closes over the new `id` automatically. The factory is cheap; re-creating it on every render is fine. - **Hard-coding the value.** `() => handleCheckChange(fixedId)` is a curried handler that always ignores the per-item value. The factory pattern loses its purpose when the closed-over value is constant."
