        "total_duration_seconds": 1069,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "You already know how to keep state in a component with `useState`, how to push it down with props, and how to share it across a subtree with `useContext`. That triangle covers a lot of apps. It starts to creak in three places:  1. The state has to live at the top of the tree, so every component in between re-renders when the value changes (unless you split contexts or memoize carefully). 2. The state only exists inside React. Anything outside React, a browser event listener, a WebSocket callback, a `setInterval`, has to reach back through a hook to read or update it. 3. The provider needs to wrap every consumer. A second copy of the same app (a second modal root, a second storybook story, a route-level sub-tree) needs its own provider, with its own state.",
          "mental_model": "A store that lives outside React is the right answer when state has to survive the absence of a component (a WebSocket handler that updates the chat when the chat panel is unmounted), when the same state has to be read by a worker or a non-React widget, or when a second tree needs its own copy without rebuilding the provider tree.  The class of bug this prevents is \"the value in the sidebar does not match the value in the URL bar because one reads a stale prop and the other reads a stale context\". With Zustand, both read the same store, both update it the same way, and there is no second copy to drift.",
          "code_example": "import { create } from \"zustand\";\n\nconst useCounterStore = create((set) => ({\n  count: 0,\n  inc: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count + 1 })),\n}));",
          "pitfalls": "Direct DOM mutation or impure side effects during the render phase cause hydration mismatches, duplicate executions in Strict Mode, and stale closures. Always declare full dependency arrays in useEffect and never read state during render from outside the component tree."
        },
