        "id": "03-003",
        "number": 3,
        "title": "`createWithEqualityFn` API reference",
        "local_chapter_file": "003-createwithequalityfn-api-reference.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "1. Hook: a comparator at the door",
          "2. The concept",
          "3. Minimal example",
          "4. Predict the render",
          "5. Common mistakes",
          "6. Why this matters"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 995,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Imagine a coffee shop with a single host stand. Each customer walking in is checked against the guest list. Some days the host only checks names (`Object.is`); some days the host checks the whole party (`shallow` on a wrapper object). In the Zustand v5 world, the default host is strict (`Object.is`), and every selector inside `useStore` is on its own: each one has to remember to bring its own custom comparator (`useShallow`) or risk kicking off an infinite loop.  `createWithEqualityFn` from `zustand/traditional` puts the host policy back at the door. You give the store one default comparator when you create it, and every selector read through the returned hook inherits that comparator automatically. You stop wrapping every selector with `useShallow`; you stop hand-rolling comparators at every call site. The comparator lives at the store, the way the host policy lives at the stand.",
          "mental_model": "Two stores in the same app can carry two different comparators. A `useBookmarkStore` created with `shallow` is friendly to wrapper selectors; a `useMetricsStore` created with the default `Object.is` is strict. Each comparator reflects the shape of the data the store owns. `createWithEqualityFn` lets you declare that policy once, at the store, instead of repeating it at every `useShallow` call.  You will see this pattern in Zustand shops that bundle many stores: each one picks the comparator that matches its data shape, and consumers do not need to know which comparator is in use. The same idea shows up in Reselect for Redux, in `useSyncExternalStoreWithSelector` for low-level external stores, and in `useMemo` with a custom comparator in vanilla React.",
          "code_example": "bun add zustand use-sync-external-store",
          "pitfalls": "- **Forgetting the peer dependency.** On a fresh Vite + React 18 setup, importing `zustand/traditional` without `use-sync-external-store` throws `Cannot find module 'use-sync-external-store/shim/...'` at build time. `bun add use-sync-external-store` once and it stops. - **Wrapping the store with `useShallow` anyway.** Once the comparator lives in the store, wrapping the selector in `useShallow` is redundant and clutters the call site. Either pick `createWithEqualityFn(..., shallow)` or pick `create` + `useShallow` per call site, not both. - **Expecting `shallow` to deep-compare nested objects.** The same rule from the previous chapter applies here. `shallow({ a: { b: 1 } }, { a: { b: 1 } })` returns `false`. The store-level comparator changes nothing about what `shallow` itself walks. - **Mixing `createWithEqualityFn` with `subscribeWithSelector` and the old v4 `subscribe(selector, listener)` overload.** v5 reorganised subscriptions; the selector overload now lives behind the `subscribeWithSelector` middleware. If you want to subscribe externally with a selector, opt in explicitly."
        },
