        "id": "03-005",
        "number": 5,
        "title": "`useStoreWithEqualityFn` hook API reference",
        "local_chapter_file": "005-usestorewithequalityfn-hook-api-reference.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "1. Hook: a vanilla store with a React-sized viewing window",
          "2. The concept",
          "3. Minimal example",
          "4. Predict the render",
          "5. Common mistakes",
          "6. Why this matters"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 1227,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Imagine a warehouse shared by several teams. The warehouse is the store. A team does not need every box delivered to its desk, so it asks for a view, such as `{ selectedIds, toggle }`. At the loading dock, a checker compares the old view and the new view. If the selected fields are unchanged, the desk stays quiet. `useStoreWithEqualityFn` lets that team choose the checker.",
          "mental_model": "This hook is the boundary between React and stores that also serve workers, tests, server-side factories, or non-React code. It prevents two opposite classes of bugs: a fresh composite selector can cause unnecessary updates or a v5 update loop under `Object.is`, while in-place `Map` and `Set` mutation can hide a real update.  A vanilla store also makes scope explicit. A provider can create one store per request, tab, route, or editor key, and a component can subscribe to only the selected slice. Keep the store instance stable with `useState`; creating it during every render loses the subscription's identity.",
          "code_example": "import { createStore } from \"zustand/vanilla\";\nimport { shallow } from \"zustand/vanilla/shallow\";\nimport { useStoreWithEqualityFn } from \"zustand/traditional\";\n\ntype CounterStore = {\n  count: number;\n  label: string;\n  increment: () => void;\n};\n\nconst counterStore = createStore<CounterStore>()((set) => ({\n  count: 0,\n  label: \"Inbox\",\n  increment: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count + 1 })),\n}));\n\nexport function Counter(): React.JSX.Element {\n  const view = useStoreWithEqualityFn(\n    counterStore,\n    (state) => ({ count: state.count, label: state.label }),\n    shallow,\n  );\n\n  return (\n    <button type=\"button\" onClick={counterStore.getState().increment}>\n      {view.label}: {view.count}\n    </button>\n  );\n}",
          "pitfalls": "- **Using `create` when you need a vanilla `StoreApi`.** `useStoreWithEqualityFn(useCounterStore, selector, shallow)` produces a type error such as `Argument of type 'UseBoundStore<StoreApi<CounterStore>>' is not assignable to parameter of type 'StoreApi<...>'`. Pass a store returned by `createStore`, or use the bound hook returned by `create`. - **Mutating a `Map` or `Set` in place.** `state.tags.set(\"react\", 1)` keeps the same reference, so subscribers can miss the change. Return `new Map(state.tags).set(\"react\", 1)` or `new Set(state.tags).add(\"react\")`. - **Leaving an empty collection untyped.** `new Set([])` can infer `Set<never>`, causing `Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'never'`. Write `new Set<string>()` or `new Set([] as string[])`. - **Replacing a store with a partial object.** In Zustand v5, `store.setState({}, true)` fails with `Argument of type '{}' is not assignable to parameter of type 'State'`. The `true` flag means replacement, so pass a complete state object. Use the default merge form for a partial update."
        },
