              "mental_model": "You will not write a custom mutator most weeks. You *will* read these tuples constantly: every time you split a store into slices, type a store that stacks `devtools` + `persist` + `immer`, or debug why `set`'s third argument stopped compiling. Knowing that `Mis` shapes the tools you receive and `Mos` advertises what you add turns those cryptic `StateCreator<S, [['zustand/devtools', never]], []>` signatures into something you can write from memory.  When you do author middleware, higher-kinded mutators are the difference between a middleware that lies about its store and one whose consumers get autocomplete for the fields it attaches. The bug class they prevent is the worst kind: code that type-checks but reaches for `store.persist` (or a devtools label, or an `immer` draft) that the types quietly stripped away, only to fail at runtime in production.",
