        "title": "Forms with React Hook Form",
        "local_chapter_file": "001-forms-with-rhf.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The concept",
          "Predict the render",
          "Sharing a form with nested components",
          "Controlled components and declarative submission",
          "Common mistakes",
          "Why this matters"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 1027,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "A paper form has a useful division of labor. The paper holds the answers, while a clerk keeps the checklist, decides whether the form is complete, and delivers it when you sign. React Hook Form (RHF) gives the browser's native inputs that first job and keeps the checklist and submission workflow outside React state.",
          "mental_model": "RHF appears in signup, checkout, settings, and search forms. Its field registration avoids a React state update for every native keystroke, while `formState` lets an error message, submit button, or field wrapper subscribe to exactly the state it displays. Typed field paths catch mismatches between a form's data model and its JSX before a request reaches an API. `Controller` keeps a third-party widget from fighting the form's value ownership. These choices prevent stale submissions, missing fields, validation bypasses, and unnecessary render work.",
          "code_example": "import { useForm, type SubmitHandler } from \"react-hook-form\";\n\ntype Signup = { email: string };\n\nexport function SignupForm() {\n  const { register, handleSubmit } = useForm<Signup>();\n  const onSubmit: SubmitHandler<Signup> = (data) => {\n    console.log(data.email);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>\n      <label>\n        Email\n        <input type=\"email\" {...register(\"email\")} />\n      </label>\n      <button type=\"submit\">Join</button>\n    </form>\n  );\n}",
          "pitfalls": "1. **Calling the handler directly.** `onSubmit={onSubmit}` skips RHF's validation wrapper. Use `onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}`. Otherwise invalid data can reach application code. 2. **Forgetting the spread.** `register(\"email\")` alone does not attach the returned `ref` and handlers. If you write `<input {...register(\"email\")} />` as `<input register={register(\"email\")} />`, TypeScript reports `Property 'register' does not exist on type 'DetailedHTMLProps<InputHTMLAttributes<HTMLInputElement>, HTMLInputElement>'.` Spread the result. 3. **Using a misspelled path.** With `useForm<Profile>()`, `register(\"acount.email\")` produces `Argument of type '\"acount.email\"' is not assignable to parameter of type '\"account\" | \"account.email\"'.` Correct the path or correct the model. 4. **Expecting `getValues` to repaint text.** `getValues` reads without subscribing. Use `watch(\"email\")` when a preview must change while typing."
        },
