              "mental_model": "Think of a Tailwind class as a labelled dial. Turning the `float-right` dial pushes an element to the right edge of its container and lets the following text wrap around it. Turning the `font-bold` dial cranks the weight to 700. Each dial does one thing. You get a layout by turning several dials at once, all through the element's class list.  In HTML you would write `class=\"float-right\"`. In React you write `className=\"float-right\"`, because `class` is a reserved word in JavaScript and JSX renames the attribute. That single rename is the only translation you need: every Tailwind class name is identical between the docs and your JSX.",
              "code_example": "// HTML from the Tailwind docs:      <p class=\"font-bold\">\n// The same thing in a React component:\n<p className=\"font-bold\">The quick brown fox.</p>",
