        "id": "05-003",
        "number": 3,
        "title": "Install Tailwind CSS in Meteor (PostCSS plugin)",
        "local_chapter_file": "003-install-tailwind-css-in-meteor-postcss-plugin.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The mental model",
          "Install Tailwind in Meteor",
          "Install Tailwind in Next.js",
          "Predict the render",
          "Utility reference",
          "Common mistakes"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 318,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Tailwind drops into a Meteor project the same way it drops into any PostCSS-based stack: install the package, register the plugin, import the CSS, start the server. There is no Meteor-specific wrapper. The recipe, plus a worked example installing the same plugin in a Next.js project, is the first half of this chapter. The second half is a working reference for a cluster of utility classes you reach for constantly: `line-clamp`, `max-w-*`, `min-h-*`, `min-w-*`, `object-fit`, `opacity`, `outline-color`, `outline-style`, `overflow`, `padding`, `position`, and `resize`.",
          "mental_model": "A PostCSS plugin transforms a CSS file before the browser sees it. `@tailwindcss/postcss` is a transformer that takes a single `@import \"tailwindcss\";` line and emits the full preflight reset plus every utility class that the build can reach from your source files. Meteor's build pipeline already runs PostCSS on your CSS assets, so registering the plugin turns the CSS entry into a Tailwind entry with no extra tooling.  In React, every utility class is identical between the HTML docs and your JSX. The only translation is the attribute name: `class` in HTML becomes `className` in JSX because `class` is reserved.",
          "code_example": "// HTML from the Tailwind docs:     <h1 class=\"text-3xl font-bold underline\">\n// The same thing in a React component:\n<h1 className=\"text-3xl font-bold underline\">Hello world!</h1>",
          "pitfalls": "React warns in the console: `Warning: Invalid DOM property 'class'. Did you mean 'className'?`. The underline never applies. Every Tailwind snippet you copy from the docs uses `class`; rename it to `className` in JSX.  If the import is not in a file the build pulls in, none of the utility classes are emitted. The plugin has no source to scan. Symptom: every `className=` value is rendered, but the element looks unstyled. Fix: add the import to the entry CSS file the framework already loads.  Meteor and Next.js both look for the PostCSS config at the project root. A config inside `src/` or `app/` is ignored. The plugin registers, but PostCSS uses its default empty plugin list. Symptom: `tailwindcss` is installed but the build pipeline does not run it. Fix: move `postcss.config.mjs` to the root.  `object-fit` only applies to `<img>`, `<video>`, `<picture>`, and friends. To cover a non-replaced element, use `bg-cover` instead.  The browser draws a default focus ring unless you opt out. `outline-none` opts out and gives you nothing in return. Replace it with `outline-hidden` (which keeps a transparent outline for forced-colors) plus a custom ring, or with `focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-blue-500`."
        },
