        "id": "05-004",
        "number": 4,
        "title": "scale utilities",
        "local_chapter_file": "004-scale-utilities.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "`scale`: growing and shrinking",
          "`scroll-behavior`: instant or animated programmatic scrolling",
          "`skew`: slanting an element",
          "`stroke`: SVG outline colour",
          "Tailwind v4 browser targets",
          "Installing Tailwind in non-Vite stacks"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 197,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "A Tailwind utility is a class name that maps to exactly one CSS declaration. `scale-110` sets `transform: scale(1.10)`. `skew-y-3` sets `transform: skewY(3deg)`. `scroll-smooth` sets `scroll-behavior: smooth`. You do not write the CSS by hand; you compose class names on the element and the browser applies the declarations they stand for.  This chapter is a working reference for four transform-flavoured utilities you reach for when building interactive UI (`scale`, `scroll-behavior`, `skew`, `stroke`), plus the tooling that ships alongside them: Tailwind v4's browser targets, the install recipes for non-Vite stacks, the VS Code IntelliSense extension, the major version feature history (v1.9, v2, v3, v4), and the \"From Zero to Production\" screencast series.",
          "mental_model": "These four utilities are the cheap-interactive-UI toolkit. A button that pops on hover is `hover:scale-110 transition-transform`. An anchor that animates instead of jumping is `scroll-smooth` on the container. An SVG icon that inherits the button's text colour is `stroke-current`. A \"next\" arrow that mirrors a previous one is `-scale-x-100`. Compose them on the same element and you get an interactive component without writing a single line of CSS.  The rest of the chapter (browser targets, install paths, IntelliSense, release history) is the meta layer that keeps the toolkit healthy. v4's modern-browser target is the reason these utilities feel fast: no autoprefixer lag, no legacy fallbacks, no polyfills. The IntelliSense extension is the reason the utilities feel fast to write: autocomplete, lint, and quick-fixes right inside the editor.",
          "code_example": "// HTML from the Tailwind docs:      <button class=\"scale-105 hover:scale-110\">\n// The same thing in a React component:\n<button className=\"scale-105 hover:scale-110 transition-transform\">\n  Save\n</button>",
          "pitfalls": "`hover:scale-110` writes `transform: scale(1.10)` on hover, with no transition. Add `transition` or `transition-transform` to animate it. The default transition duration is 150 ms and the default property list is the seven common transform / colour properties; `transition-transform` is the right shorthand for a transform-only animation.  `scale-z-150` only affects rendering when the element is in a 3D context (it has a parent with `transform-style: preserve-3d`, or its own `perspective-*` plus a 3D transform). On a flat element, `scale-z-150` produces no visible change. Pair it with `scale-3d` and a `perspective-*` utility on an ancestor if you want a real 3D effect.  `skew-y-6` pivots around the element's centre by default. If you wanted the parallelogram to lean on its bottom edge, add `origin-bottom` (or `origin-bottom-left` for a sharper tilt). The skew itself is correct; the visible result is off because the rotation pivot is wrong.  `scroll-smooth` only animates *programmatic* scrolling: anchor navigation, `scrollIntoView()`, `scrollTo()`, the URL hash jump. Mouse wheel, touch, and scroll bar drags are unaffected. If a user complaint is \"scrolling feels jerky,\" that is a scroll container issue, not a `scroll-behavior` issue.  Tailwind v4 expects to *be* the CSS preprocessor. If your project still runs Sass, the two engines will fight over `@import` resolution and source-map paths. Either migrate the Sass files to plain CSS (Tailwind v4 handles nesting via native CSS `@nest`) or downgrade to Tailwind v3."
        },
