        "id": "05-008",
        "number": 8,
        "title": "Tailwind color palette",
        "local_chapter_file": "008-tailwind-color-palette.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The mental model: a name, a step, and a variable",
          "One palette, many properties",
          "Opacity: the slash modifier",
          "Dark mode: the `dark:` variant",
          "Referencing colors in CSS",
          "Customizing the palette with `@theme`"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 717,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "A color utility in Tailwind is a class name that resolves to one CSS variable. `bg-sky-500` sets `background-color: var(--color-sky-500)`. `text-gray-950` sets `color: var(--color-gray-950)`. `border-pink-300` sets `border-color: var(--color-pink-300)`. You never write a hex value on the element. You pick a color name and a step, and the class resolves to a design-token variable the framework already defined.  This chapter is the color layer of Tailwind plus a small group of interactivity and layout utilities that come up in the same components: the multi-step palette and its opacity, CSS variable, and dark-mode features; the `cursor-*`, `pointer-events-*`, and `resize-*` interactivity utilities; the `display` utilities; and two pieces of Tailwind ecosystem context (the IntelliSense editor extension and the Tailwind UI → Tailwind Plus rebrand).",
          "mental_model": "Every color in the default palette has a name (`red`, `sky`, `slate`, `emerald`, …) and **11 steps**: `50`, `100`, `200`, `300`, `400`, `500`, `600`, `700`, `800`, `900`, `950`. Step `50` is the lightest, `950` is the darkest, and `500` sits in the middle as the \"base\" of the ramp. A color utility is the name and the step glued together behind a property prefix:  The steps are defined in the OKLCH color space, not RGB or HSL. OKLCH is perceptually uniform: stepping from `400` to `500` looks like the same jump in lightness whether the hue is yellow or blue, which is why the ramps look even across every color. You do not need to write OKLCH yourself. You pick the step; the framework holds the value.",
          "code_example": "// HTML in the Tailwind docs:  <div class=\"bg-sky-500 text-white\">\n// The same thing in React:\n<div className=\"bg-sky-500 text-white\">...</div>",
          "pitfalls": "Tailwind generates CSS by scanning your source for complete class strings. `\"bg-\" + color + \"-500\"` is never a complete string in the file, so `bg-sky-500` is never generated and the element renders unstyled. The fix is to write whole class names and switch between them, or to constrain the dynamic part to a small typed union and interpolate that (`bg-${tone}-500` where `tone` is `\"sky\" | \"rose\"`) so every possible result is spelled out somewhere the scanner can reach. When in doubt, map the input to a lookup of literal class strings.  `opacity-50` makes the whole element (background *and* its text and children) 50% transparent. The slash modifier `bg-sky-500/50` changes only the background color's alpha and leaves the text fully opaque. For a translucent card or scrim with readable content, you almost always want the slash, not `opacity-*`.  A \"saved!\" confirmation that toggles between `hidden` and `block` makes the layout jump every time it appears, because the element goes from taking no space to taking its full space. If the surrounding layout must stay stable, toggle `invisible` / `visible` instead: the space is always reserved and only the paint changes.  An icon absolutely-positioned over an input steals the click unless it opts out of hit testing. Add `pointer-events-none` to the icon so the click falls through and focuses the input. Conversely, if an interactive overlay is not responding, check that a parent did not set `pointer-events-none` and swallow the events.  `dark:bg-gray-800` is not a runtime conditional in your component. It compiles to a CSS rule that applies under the dark-mode selector. You do not read a \"theme\" value in the component to choose it; you write both branches as utilities and the browser resolves which one applies. Toggling the theme is a separate concern (a class on `<html>` or the OS setting), not something each component decides."
        },
