        "id": "05-009",
        "number": 9,
        "title": "Width and height utilities",
        "local_chapter_file": "009-width-and-height-utilities.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The mental model: a spacing scale, plus a few kernel-level utilities",
          "`w-*`: width from the spacing scale",
          "`h-*`: height from the spacing scale",
          "`size-*`: shorthand for width and height",
          "`min-w-*`, `max-w-*`, `min-h-*`, `max-h-*`: bounding the box",
          "Automatic class detection and static class names"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 356,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "A Tailwind utility is a class name that maps to one CSS declaration. `w-12` sets `width: calc(var(--spacing) * 12)`. `h-screen` sets `height: 100vh`. `size-6` sets both axes to the same value. You compose class names on the element and the browser applies the declarations they stand for.  This chapter is a reference roundup. The top of the chapter covers the width and height family (`w-*`, `min-w-*`, `max-w-*`, `h-*`, `min-h-*`, `max-h-*`, `size-*`). The middle covers three utility groups that often show up next to sizing: `break-before-*` / `break-inside-*` for print and columns, `clear-*` for float wrapping, and Tailwind's static-class detection rule that decides whether a runtime-built class name will appear in the generated CSS. The tail covers a small set of one-off utilities (font features, font stretch, grid auto flow, direction-aware text alignment, composable gradients) and a few Tailwind UI product primers (the single-page changelog template, framework install guides for non-React stacks, the design-engineering job description).",
          "mental_model": "Tailwind v4 derives every numeric sizing utility from a single CSS variable: `--spacing`. The default value is `0.25rem`, so `w-1` is `0.25rem`, `w-4` is `1rem`, `w-96` is `24rem`. The `h-*`, `min-*`, `max-*`, and `size-*` families are the same dial in different domains: horizontal, vertical, bounded, and symmetric sizing.  The other utilities in this chapter are independent kernels: `break-*` is CSS paged-media, `clear-*` is float layout, `font-stretch` and `font-feature-settings` are OpenType selection, `grid-flow-*` is grid auto-placement, `text-start` / `text-end` are logical alignment, `bg-gradient-to-*` plus `from-*` / `via-*` / `to-*` plus `bg-clip-text` are layered backgrounds. Each is a small, complete API you can read independently.",
          "code_example": "// HTML from the Tailwind docs:     <div class=\"w-32 h-12\">\n// The same thing in a React component:\n<div className=\"w-32 h-12\">...</div>",
          "pitfalls": "Direct DOM mutation or impure side effects during the render phase cause hydration mismatches, duplicate executions in Strict Mode, and stale closures. Always declare full dependency arrays in useEffect and never read state during render from outside the component tree."
        },
