        "id": "05-011",
        "number": 11,
        "title": "Overscroll-behavior utilities",
        "local_chapter_file": "011-overscroll-behavior-utilities.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Mental model: what reaching the edge means",
          "`overscroll-*` and `overscroll-x-*` / `overscroll-y-*`",
          "`pointer-events-*`: block or pass through pointer input",
          "Positioning: `static`, `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, `sticky`",
          "`scrollbar-*`: thickness and visibility",
          "The `@tailwindcss/typography` plugin and `prose`"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 526,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "When a scrollable element reaches its edge, three things can happen: it chains the scroll up to a parent container, it bounces within its own bounds (the iOS-style rubber-band effect), or it does nothing at all. Tailwind v4 exposes those three behaviours as `overscroll-auto`, `overscroll-contain`, and `overscroll-none`. The same property has axis variants (`overscroll-x-*`, `overscroll-y-*`) for the rare case where horizontal and vertical scrolling need different rules. Once you can name what each value does, a long-standing source of scroll bugs in modals, sidebars, and nested carousels turns into a one-line class.  The chapter is a reference roundup. The top half is the overscroll family and its neighbours that you reach for on the same scroll panel: `pointer-events-*`, the Tailwind positioning utilities (`static`, `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, `sticky`), and the `scrollbar-*` utilities (`auto`, `thin`, `none`). The second half covers three more groups that share the scroll / viewport mental model: the `@tailwindcss/typography` plugin's `prose` classes for long-form content, the scroll-snap family (`snap-*`, `snap-stop-*`, `scroll-m-*`, `scroll-p-*`), the `rotate-*` transform utilities, and the React-level rule for encapsulating repeated Tailwind combinations. The tail lists two Tailwind UI templates (Protocol, Radiant) as starting points rather than utilities, with a note that they are paid products and out of scope for a course on writing classes.",
          "mental_model": "The browser's `overscroll-behavior` controls three independent choices at the boundary of a scrollable element:  The reason the same property fixes several common UX bugs:",
          "code_example": "<div className=\"overscroll-contain h-64 overflow-y-auto\">…</div>",
          "pitfalls": "`scroll-snap-align` on a child is a no-op without `scroll-snap-type` on a scrollable parent. The common symptom is \"I added `snap-start` to my images and the scroll does not snap.\" The fix: add `snap-x-mandatory` (or `snap-x-proximity`) on the scroll container.  `scroll-m-*` lives on the *child*; `scroll-p-*` lives on the *container*. Putting `scroll-m-4` on the container and `scroll-p-4` on the child reverses which axis offsets the snap. Pick by which element's geometry should be inset.  `overscroll-behavior` has no effect unless the element actually overflows. Wrap the contents with `overflow-y-auto` (or `overflow-x-auto` for horizontal) for the property to bite. The CSS is ignored otherwise.  `position: sticky` does not work when the nearest scrolling ancestor is also an `overflow: hidden` ancestor; the sticky element sticks relative to the wrong box and appears to not stick at all. If a sticky header does not stick, walk up the tree for an `overflow-hidden` parent and replace it with `overflow-clip` (which lets sticky work) or remove the overflow rule.  Tailwind's scanner reads source as plain text and generates CSS only for complete class-name strings. It cannot follow a template literal interpolation. Use a static lookup object (`{ sm: \"w-32\", md: \"w-64\" }`) or write the *complete* arbitrary value (`w-[var(--card-w)]`) so the class name is a single, scannable string."
        },
