        "id": "05-015",
        "number": 15,
        "title": "Tailwind theme variables",
        "local_chapter_file": "015-tailwind-theme-variables.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The `@theme` contract",
          "Theme variables are real CSS custom properties",
          "`@theme` vs `@theme inline`",
          "The v1.9 `presets` story (legacy)",
          "The v2.1 JIT engine promotion",
          "The v2.2 surface: pseudo-elements, typography, and slash opacity"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 954,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 2 · **Prerequisites:** Tailwind CSS: Utility-First Styling, Tailwind UI Ecommerce kit  Theme variables are the design-token layer of Tailwind v4. A `@theme` block declares CSS custom properties such as `--color-mint-500`, `--font-display`, or `--breakpoint-3xl`. Each one does two jobs at once: it becomes a real CSS custom property on `:root` that any stylesheet can read with `var(...)`, and it tells the JIT engine which utilities and variants to generate. The takeaway is that there is no separate \"config\" step: declare the token, get the utilities.",
          "mental_model": "Theme variables are the bridge between \"Tailwind as a utility catalogue\" and \"Tailwind as a design-system platform\". Once a team codifies its colours, fonts, spacings, and breakpoints in a shared `@theme` block (or publishes a package that exports one), every utility becomes a token reference. The class name names the design decision, and the design decision lives in one place.  You will meet the surrounding tooling everywhere in real codebases: the `@tailwindcss/vite` plugin in a fresh Vite app, the `@tailwindcss/postcss` plugin in a Webpack migration, the React framework guide pinning Tailwind to a particular bundler, the v2.2 `before:` / `selection:` / `/<opacity>` families surviving into v4 because they are load-bearing for any UI with quotes, bullets, or translucent overlays, the v1.9 `presets` key in a config from the v2 era that someone forgot to migrate. The release history is the reason a snippet you copy from a 2022 blog post still works on a 2026 codebase: the class names survived even when the engine underneath changed twice.",
          "code_example": "@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n@theme {\n  --color-mint-500: oklch(0.72 0.15 165);\n  --font-display: \"Inter\", \"system-ui\", \"sans-serif\";\n  --breakpoint-3xl: 1920px;\n}",
          "pitfalls": "In v4 the source of truth is the `@theme` block. Grepping the config file for `--color-brand-500` returns nothing even when the brand colours render correctly. If a colour is unexplained, look at the CSS first.  Without the reset, Tailwind ships the full default palette (`slate`, `gray`, `red`, ...). Drop `--color-*: initial;` at the top of your `@theme` block to wipe the defaults and only emit the tokens you actually declare. Useful for strict design-system gates that forbid untokenised colours.  The `before:` and `after:` variants are designed for purely decorative glyphs (quote marks, icons, list markers). Writing a `<span>` for the same effect adds a DOM node a screen reader can reach, a `key`-collision source, and an extra element you must keep styling. Reach for `before:` first.  The hint costs memory per element. Apply it to the few elements that are about to animate, then remove the class when the animation finishes. A `will-change-*` blanket is a perf regression, not a fix.  The opposite of the fix you wanted. `touch-pan-x` claims horizontal pan only, leaving vertical pan free for the browser to interpret. For a vertical scroller that the browser keeps hijacking (overscroll, pull-to-refresh) use `touch-pan-y`. For full ownership of every gesture (a custom map, a drawing canvas, a game) use `touch-none`."
        },
