          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 1-2 · **Prerequisites:** stroke, Tailwind CSS: Utility-First Styling, Outline styles, Tailwind theme variables, Custom color tokens  The chapter title picks up a small but useful CSS trick: `content-[attr(name)]` reads a DOM attribute straight into a pseudo-element's generated text. Once you can do that, the rest of the chapter is the supporting vocabulary that tends to show up next to it: native form state variants (`required`, `invalid`, `checked`, `disabled`) so the browser does the conditional class work for you, perspective utilities for 3D transforms, responsive breakpoints and breakpoint configuration, accessible outline utilities, text color opacity modifiers, transform composition and reset, the `color-scheme` utilities, visual effects, white-space preservation, sizing and width, and the table-layout pair. One chapter, many small utility families, all already in the `theme` namespace you built earlier.",
          "mental_model": "Generated content from attributes is the cheapest way to render a label without changing the markup. Form state variants keep the conditional class logic out of the template: the browser picks the rule, not the JSX. Perspective utilities, transform composition, and effects utility handle the 3D and visual polish. Text color opacity, white-space, sizing, and table layout are the small utilities you reach for on every paragraph, every label, every form field, every data grid. Once you can read and write them, the rest of the chapter's families are the same pattern: a short list of complete literal classes, one or two CSS keywords, and a worked example that combines them.",
          "code_example": "<a\n  href=\"/docs/hooks\"\n  data-section=\"Hooks\"\n  className=\"relative no-underline hover:underline before:absolute before:-top-2 before:left-0 before:text-[10px] before:font-semibold before:uppercase before:tracking-widest before:text-rose-500 before:content-[attr(data-section)]\"\n>\n  useState\n</a>",
          "pitfalls": "1. **Forgetting that `content-[attr(name)]` reads the attribute value literally, without CSS string interpolation.** The pseudo-element prints whatever the attribute value is, character for character. If the attribute is missing, the content is empty. There is no default fallback. 2. **Using `attr(name)` without a fallback for missing attributes.** If the attribute is not on the element, the pseudo-element renders nothing. Either declare the attribute on every element or pair `content-[attr(name)]` with a CSS-only fallback (uncommon, just always declare the attribute). 3. **Assuming `invalid:` only fires after form submission.** `:invalid` is live: it fires the moment the typed value fails the browser's constraint validation. That's the right behavior for a \"show the user what's wrong\" UI, but it also means the input is rose from the very first keystroke if the field is `required` and the value is empty. 4. **Applying `perspective-*` to the element being rotated.** The perspective belongs on the **parent** of the 3D-transformed element. Putting it on the rotated element itself does nothing for the transform, because the perspective reference for the child is the element's own box, which gets clipped by the rotation. 5. **Forgetting to add `transform-style: preserve-3d` to the rotated child.** Without `preserve-3d`, the child's own 3D children flatten to the parent's plane. There is no Tailwind utility for this; keep it as an inline `style={{ transformStyle: \"preserve-3d\" }}` or a custom class. 6. **Stacking `max-md:` with `md:` on the same utility.** The two variants are mutually exclusive: `max-md:` is the media range below 768px, `md:` is the range at 768px and up. The browser uses the last matching rule, so the order in the className matters and the visual can flip from one media query to the next. Pick one breakpoint system per utility. 7. **Using `outline-none` and thinking the focus ring is gone.** Tailwind's `outline-none` is a transparent outline that high-c"
