              "mental_model": "A box on the page has three independent sizing decisions: how big it is by default (`size-*`, `w-*`, `h-*`), how it can stretch or shrink (`min-*`, `max-*`), and how its replaced content (an `<img>`, `<video>`, `<svg>`) fits inside the box it has been given (`object-fit`, `object-position`, `aspect-*`). Each layer composes with the next.  Logical-property utilities (`inline-size-*`, `block-size-*`) are the direction-aware replacements for the physical `width-*` / `height-*` family. They map to the inline axis (the direction text flows) and the block axis (the direction lines stack) respectively, so the same component lays out correctly in LTR and RTL documents without a conditional. For most React codebases that ship only in one direction, `w-*` and `h-*` are simpler and identical; reach for `inline-size-*` / `block-size-*` when a component needs to survive a locale change.",
