        "id": "05-024",
        "number": 24,
        "title": "Tailwind justify-items utilities",
        "local_chapter_file": "024-tailwind-justify-items-utilities.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The mental model: two axes, two endpoints, one override slot",
          "`justify-*`: position items along the main axis",
          "`items-*`: align items along the cross axis",
          "`justify-items-*`: align every grid item along the inline axis",
          "`self-*`: override cross-axis alignment for one item",
          "`justify-self-*`: override inline-axis alignment for one grid item"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 343,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Tailwind's flex and grid utilities are a thin vocabulary over the CSS box model. Each class name maps to one CSS declaration: `justify-center` is `justify-content: center`, `items-start` is `align-items: flex-start`, `order-2` is `order: 2`, `space-x-4` is `margin-left: 1rem` on every child after the first. You compose class names on the element and the browser applies the declarations they stand for.  This chapter is a reference roundup. The first half covers the flex/grid alignment families in two pairs: container-side (`justify-content`, `align-items`, `justify-items`) and item-side (`justify-self`, `align-self`, `order`). The middle covers spacing siblings (`space-x-*`, `space-y-*`), the flex sizing primitives (`flex`, `flex-1`, `grow`, `shrink`), and grid template tracks (`grid-cols-*`, `grid-rows-*`). The tail covers multi-column flow (`columns-*`) and the `break-*` family that controls whether pages or columns may split an element.",
          "mental_model": "CSS flex and grid layout both describe how items sit inside a container. The container has a main axis (the direction the items are laid out) and a cross axis (the perpendicular direction). For each axis you have two kinds of choices: a container-side rule that applies to every item, and an item-side rule that overrides the container for one item.  Tailwind gives each of these slots a utility family. The container-side families end in a plural or a generic verb (`justify-*`, `items-*`, `justify-items-*`). The item-side families end in `self-*` (`justify-self-*`, `self-*`). The `order-*` family sits outside the axis model: it changes the visual order of items without touching the source order in the DOM.",
          "code_example": "// HTML from the Tailwind docs: <div class=\"flex items-center justify-between\">\n// The same thing in a React component:\n<div className=\"flex items-center justify-between\">...</div>",
          "pitfalls": "`break-inside-*` controls whether a page or column may split the contents of an element. `break-after-*` controls whether a page or column break is created immediately after an element. The values: `auto` (default), `avoid` (no break), `avoid-page`, `avoid-column`, `page`, `column`.  `break-inside-avoid` on a figure inside a `columns-3` article keeps the figure intact in one column, then resumes the text flow in the next. Without it, the browser may split the figure between two columns."
        },
