        "id": "05-025",
        "number": 25,
        "title": "Break before an element",
        "local_chapter_file": "025-break-before-an-element.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Mental model: breaks and two-axis shortcuts",
          "`break-before-*` and `break-after-*`: force a break, then resume",
          "`place-content-*`: two-axis content distribution for the container",
          "`place-items-*`: set per-cell alignment once on the container",
          "`place-self-*`: override container alignment for one item",
          "`space-x-*` / `space-y-*` vs `gap`: when each one is right"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 375,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 2-3 · **Prerequisites:** Tailwind justify-items utilities, Z-axis translation, Generated content from attributes, scale utilities, Tailwind UI Ecommerce kit, Tailwind theme variables, stroke  The headline concept is `break-before-*`, the family of utilities that ask the browser to start the next page, column, or region on a fresh break right before an element. Everything else in this chapter is a Tailwind v4 reference roundup: `break-after-*` complements it, and a block of two-axis alignment shortcuts (`place-content-*`, `place-items-*`, `place-self-*`) replaces the verbose `justify-items-*` plus `align-items-*` boilerplate. A short note at the end covers `space-*` versus `gap` so you stop reaching for `space-y-4` on grids, then four framework installation guides (Gatsby, Laravel + Vite, Meteor, Ember.js), a note on layer-aware purge, the `@plugin` directive for legacy Tailwind v3 plugins, and the relationship-bound state variants (`group-*`, `peer-*`, `has-*`, `aria-*`, `data-*`).",
          "mental_model": "A break (`break-before-*` / `break-after-*` / `break-inside-*`) is an instruction the layout engine obeys when it is splitting content across pages, columns, or regions. `columns-3` from chapter 024 turns an article into a three-column newspaper flow; `break-before-column` on the next `<h2>` forces that heading to start a new column. `place-content-*` and friends are vocabulary shortcuts: each one writes two CSS declarations, mirroring the relationship between `justify-content` and `align-content` on a container, or between `justify-self` and `align-self` on an item.  Every entry in this chapter is a thin layer over an existing CSS declaration. The value is not new power, it is fewer characters to type and a consistent vocabulary across the team.",
          "code_example": "<article className=\"columns-3 gap-8\">\n  <p>...</p>\n  <h2 className=\"break-before-column text-xl font-semibold mt-6\">Section A</h2>\n  <p>...</p>\n  <h2 className=\"break-before-column text-xl font-semibold mt-6\">Section B</h2>\n</article>",
          "pitfalls": "Direct DOM mutation or impure side effects during the render phase cause hydration mismatches, duplicate executions in Strict Mode, and stale closures. Always declare full dependency arrays in useEffect and never read state during render from outside the component tree."
        },
