        "total_duration_seconds": 451,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 1 · **Prerequisites:** Outline styles, Tailwind CSS: Utility-First Styling, Custom color tokens, Tailwind theme variables, CSS transition utilities, Break before an element  The outline-styles chapter taught you that an outline is a line drawn *outside* the box, invisible to layout, and the safe surface for a keyboard focus ring. This chapter zooms in on the three utilities that decide how that line looks (`outline-<width>`, `outline-<color>`, `outline-offset-<n>`), the `ring-*` alternative built from box-shadow, the `scale-*` transform, the `underline` / `line-through` text-decoration family, and the four transition utilities that animate all of it: `transition-<group>`, `duration-<n>`, `delay-<n>`, and `ease-*`. Every one of these is difficulty 1. The value is in the combinations: a focus ring that fades in over 200ms, a card that scales up on hover, a \"done\" item that strikes through with a coloured line.",
          "mental_model": "Outline width, colour, and offset are the accessibility surface: a focus ring that a keyboard user can see, that high-contrast mode can force to a system colour, and that never shifts layout when it appears. The ring utilities give you the same indicator when you want it to hug rounded corners. Scale is the hover/press affordance on every interactive card and button in a modern UI. Text-decoration lines carry meaning (a struck-through price, a completed task) that colour alone should not carry. And the four transition families are what separate a UI that snaps harshly from one that feels considered, all without a single line of JavaScript animation code. You will apply these together on nearly every button, link, and card you build for the rest of the course.",
          "code_example": "<button className=\"outline-2 outline-sky-500 outline-offset-2\">Save</button>",
          "pitfalls": "1. **`outline-2` with no colour on a light background.** The width is set, but `outline-color` defaults to the element's `currentColor` (its text colour). On a white button with dark text you get a dark ring you did not design. Always pair a width with an explicit `outline-<color>` (or `ring-<color>`). 2. **Expecting `outline-offset` to animate the box.** It animates the *gap*, not the box size, which is exactly why it is safe. If you meant to grow the element, that is `scale-*` or a size utility, not offset. 3. **Reaching for `transition-all` by default.** It animates every property, including layout properties like `height` and `width` that reflow. A hover that changes `height` under `transition-all` will visibly lurch. Use the narrowest group: `transition-colors`, `transition-transform`, `transition-shadow`. 4. **Setting `duration-*` or `ease-*` without a `transition-*` group.** `duration-300` alone does nothing; there is no transition to time. The group class (`transition`, `transition-colors`, …) is what turns animation on. Duration, easing, and delay only modify an already-declared transition. 5. **Animating a `ring` with `transition-colors`.** A ring is a box-shadow, not a colour property. To animate a ring appearing, use `transition-shadow` (or `transition`, which includes shadow). `transition-colors` will animate `ring-color` changes but not the ring *width* growing from 0. 6. **Interpolating a dynamic class like `` `outline-${n}` ``.** Tailwind's scanner only sees complete literal tokens in your source. A template-built class name never generates the CSS and the outline silently disappears. Use a lookup of full literals (`outline-2`, `outline-4`) keyed by the variable. 7. **Using `-scale-x-100` and wondering why text reads backwards.** A negative scale mirrors the element, including its text and children. Mirror only decorative elements (an icon), not content."
        },
