        "id": "05-027",
        "number": 27,
        "title": "Transition property utilities",
        "local_chapter_file": "027-transition-property-utilities.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Mental model: an accessibility-first motion vocabulary",
          "`transition-property-(*)` and `transition-[<list>]`: named property families",
          "`transition-discrete` and `transition-normal`: discrete-property behaviour",
          "`motion-reduce:` and `motion-safe:` variants",
          "`--ease-*` theme variables: custom easing as a design token",
          "`zoom-*` utilities: the CSS `zoom` property"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 565,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 1-3 · **Prerequisites:** CSS transition utilities, Break before an element, Outline styles, Outline width, Background color, image, size, and position utilities, Tailwind theme variables, stroke  This chapter is the wider Tailwind vocabulary that surrounds `transition-*` and the transform families. It collects the property-list extensions, the accessibility-aware variants, the interactivity utilities, the motion-preference variants, and the few transform-adjacent families that did not fit in the CSS transition utilities chapter. Each section is short on prose and dense on tables.",
          "mental_model": "A Tailwind UI is a contract with the user as much as it is a set of classes. Each interactive utility answers a question: *what animates*, *who gets it*, *when does it fire*, *does the user know it's moving*. The contracts layer on top of each other:  Read the class string once per layer. The first layer says what animates. The second layer says which user states it applies to. The third layer says how it degrades for users who opted out.",
          "code_example": "@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n@theme {\n  --card-properties: width, height, color, background-color, box-shadow;\n}",
          "pitfalls": "// right: branch between complete strings. className={kind === \"color\" ? \"transition-colors\" : \"transition-transform\"} ```  The full class name is never a literal in a scanned file, so the scanner does not generate CSS for it. The style silently does nothing. Branch between complete strings or build a `Record<Kind, string>` of complete class strings.  A user who has asked the OS for less motion still sees the lift. The WCAG-recommended fix is to pair every animated utility with `motion-reduce:`:  Forgetting the `motion-reduce:` on a *single* utility breaks the user's accessibility setting without warning. Audit class strings for any `transition-*`, `duration-*`, `delay-*`, `animate-*`, `scale-*`, `rotate-*`, or `translate-*`; each is a candidate.  The `focus:outline-none` removes the browser's native focus ring without providing a replacement. A keyboard user has no way to tell where focus is. The right pattern is a focus-visible rule with a visible replacement:"
        },
