        "id": "05-028",
        "number": 28,
        "title": "perspective-origin",
        "local_chapter_file": "028-perspective-origin.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Mental model: a camera aimed at a card",
          "The concept",
          "Minimal example",
          "Reference roundup: the surrounding Tailwind vocabulary",
          "Worked example: a typed 3D card and carousel",
          "Predict the render"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 408,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 1-3 · **Prerequisites:** Transition property utilities, Tailwind theme variables, stroke, Z-axis translation, Width and height utilities, Break before an element, overscroll-behavior utilities, Custom color tokens, Tailwind CSS: Utility-First Styling, Tailwind UI Ecommerce kit, Generated content from attributes  This is a reference-heavy Tailwind chapter. Its main subject is `perspective-origin`, with a compact reference roundup for related Tailwind v4 configuration and interaction utilities used in the exercises.",
          "mental_model": "Imagine a small card floating in front of a camera. `perspective-*` chooses how close the camera is. `perspective-origin-*` moves the camera's vanishing point across the card. The card can use the same `rotate-y-12` transform in both cases, yet the tilt appears to lean toward a different corner.  The origin is a coordinate on the element being projected. It does not move the element by itself. A 3D transform must be present before changing the origin can produce a visible difference.",
          "code_example": "export function OriginPreview() {\n  return (\n    <div className=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-6\">\n      <div className=\"perspective-near\">\n        <div className=\"perspective-origin-top-left transform-3d rotate-y-12 rounded-lg bg-sky-500 p-6 text-white\">\n          top left\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div className=\"perspective-near\">\n        <div className=\"perspective-origin-bottom-right transform-3d rotate-y-12 rounded-lg bg-indigo-500 p-6 text-white\">\n          bottom right\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}",
          "pitfalls": "This deliberately broken TypeScript does not compile:  function Origin({ value }: { value: string }) {   const className: string = origins[value as keyof typeof origins];   return <div className={className} />; } ```  A stricter and safer version accepts the union at the component boundary:  function Origin({ value }: { value: Origin }) {   return <div className={origins[value]} />; } ```  If you remove the cast in the broken version, TypeScript reports `Element implicitly has an 'any' type because expression of type 'string' can't be used to index type ...`. The cast can hide an invalid value, so a prop union is the better boundary."
        },
