        "id": "05-029",
        "number": 29,
        "title": "Shadows, opacity, blending, and isolation",
        "local_chapter_file": "029-shadows-opacity-blending-and-isolation.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Box shadow: elevation tokens and inset shadows",
          "Text shadow: `text-shadow-*` and shadow colour",
          "Opacity: `opacity-*` and how it differs from slash-opacity",
          "Filter family: blur, brightness, and composition",
          "Mix-blend-mode: how an element combines with its backdrop",
          "Isolation: `isolate`"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 431,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 1-3 · **Prerequisites:** Custom color tokens, perspective-origin, Outline styles, Break before an element, stroke  Effects are the surface layer of a UI. They make cards lift, text punch through a busy hero, and a frosted modal read like glass instead of cardboard. Tailwind groups all of these under one roof, calling them *effects*. Most are zero-runtime utility wrappers around a CSS property: `shadow-*` wraps `box-shadow`, `opacity-*` wraps `opacity`, `mix-blend-*` wraps `mix-blend-mode`, `mask-*` wraps the mask family, and `backdrop-*` wraps the backdrop-filter family.",
          "mental_model": "Every modern UI pulls from this toolkit: a hero headline drops a text shadow, a card lifts on hover, a modal blurs its backdrop, a price tag multiplies into the card. Treating the effect utilities as a vocabulary (a `box-shadow` story, a `text-shadow` story, an `opacity` story, a `filter` story, a `mix-blend-mode` story, a `mask` story, a `prose` story) keeps every component pulled from the same palette. The classes live in the same `className` strings as the layout utilities from earlier chapters, so a fully styled component is one string per element.",
          "code_example": "<button\n  type=\"button\"\n  className=\"rounded-md bg-white px-4 py-2 text-slate-900\n             shadow-md hover:shadow-lg\n             focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-sky-500\n             dark:bg-slate-800 dark:text-slate-100\"\n>\n  Save\n</button>",
          "pitfalls": "Tailwind's scanner does not see the assembled string, so the utility is missing from the generated CSS. The element renders with no shadow at all, and the bug surfaces only in production builds where unused utilities are stripped. Map values to complete strings instead.  `opacity` is multiplied across every rendered pixel of the element and its children. The title and the body fade with the surface. If the goal is a translucent surface with crisp text, reach for `bg-sky-500/30` instead and leave `text-*` fully opaque.  Without an isolating ancestor, `mix-blend-mode` composites against everything behind the element in the stacking context, including the page background. The multiply can produce unexpected colours when the layout shifts. Wrap the card in `isolate` (or any `position: relative` ancestor without `z-index: auto`); inside Tailwind, prefer the explicit `isolate` utility.  `transition-all` covers everything, including unintended properties (a `border-radius` change, a `padding` change). `transition-shadow` is the explicit subset that matches `box-shadow`. Pair the geometry utility with the matching transition utility.  `backdrop-filter` operates on the pixels behind the element. If the background is opaque (`bg-white`), the backdrop filter has nothing to operate on and visually does nothing. Always pair backdrop filters with translucent backgrounds (`bg-white/30`, `bg-sky-500/40`)."
        },
