        "id": "05-030",
        "number": 30,
        "title": "Tailwind contrast filter",
        "local_chapter_file": "030-tailwind-contrast-filter.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "`contrast-*`",
          "`grayscale`",
          "`hue-rotate-*`",
          "`invert`",
          "`saturate-*`",
          "`sepia`"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 318,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 1-2 · **Prerequisites:** Shadows, opacity, blending, and isolation  CSS `filter` accepts a chain of effects: `contrast(150%) grayscale(50%) hue-rotate(45deg)`. Tailwind splits the chain into single-purpose utilities that compose on the element. `backdrop-filter` is the same idea applied to whatever is behind the element, which is only visible when the element itself is translucent. `drop-shadow` is the filter-family version of `box-shadow`, and it follows the rendered silhouette instead of the box.",
          "mental_model": "Filters show up the moment a UI stops being a stack of rectangles: a hero photo that needs more punch, a logo that needs to flip in dark mode, a frosted modal scrim, a coloured drop-shadow on an SVG icon. Tailwind's filter families are the canonical place to reach for them; CSS exposes the same primitives, but the utility form lets you keep all of styling in `className` so the design tokens and the component logic live in the same file.  - `filter-*` on the element, `backdrop-*` on what is behind. They compose independently. - `drop-shadow-*` follows the silhouette; `shadow-*` follows the box. For SVG, PNG-with-transparency, or text, `drop-shadow` is correct; for cards, modals, and other rectangles, `shadow` is correct.",
          "code_example": "<img className=\"contrast-50 ...\" src=\"/photo.jpg\" alt=\"Low contrast\" />\n<img className=\"contrast-100 ...\" src=\"/photo.jpg\" alt=\"Identity\" />\n<img className=\"contrast-150 hover:contrast-200 ...\" src=\"/photo.jpg\" alt=\"Boosted\" />",
          "pitfalls": "`drop-shadow-lg` on a rectangular div produces the same result as `shadow-lg`. The cost is higher: `drop-shadow` runs through the silhouette pass even though the silhouette is the same as the box. For rectangles, `shadow-lg` is the right tool. Reach for `drop-shadow` only when the silhouette is non-rectangular: SVG icons, PNG logos with transparency, text glyphs.  `bg-white` is opaque; the backdrop blur has nothing to blur. The element looks like a plain white card. Pair every `backdrop-*` utility with a `/<opacity>` colour modifier (`bg-white/30`, `bg-slate-900/40`).  These are two different effects. `saturate-150` recolours the image pixels. `backdrop-saturate-150` recolours the pixels behind the `bg-slate-900` element. They look similar in a screenshot, but the underlying filter declarations are different and the cost profile differs.  Hue rotation works on the entire hue wheel, including greys and near-greys. A logo that mixes brand orange with a near-grey support colour will have its orange shifted but its grey left roughly where it was. For \"swap the brand colour only\", reach for an explicit recolour via CSS variables or export a second variant. Hue rotation is a quick recolour, not a brand re-skin.  The hover state should use the named `hue-rotate-0` utility if one exists, or the bare `hue-rotate-0`. The arbitrary `[0deg]` form works but is the more verbose choice. The named utilities exist precisely for these \"turn it back off\" moments: `grayscale-0`, `invert-0`, `sepia-0`, `hue-rotate-0`, `contrast-100`, `saturate-100`, `brightness-100`."
        },
