        "id": "05-032",
        "number": 32,
        "title": "Tailwind v4 default style changes",
        "local_chapter_file": "032-tailwind-v4-default-style-changes.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Hook: read the release notes before you upgrade",
          "Default style changes",
          "Individual transform properties",
          "Removed and renamed utilities",
          "Variant behavior",
          "`will-change` utilities"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 867,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 3 · **Prerequisites:** Global important mode and utility conflicts, perspective-origin, CSS transition utilities, Outline width, Break before an element, Transition property utilities, Background color, image, size, and position utilities, Tailwind CSS: Utility-First Styling, Currying event-handler factories, Tailwind theme variables, Custom color tokens, stroke  Tailwind v4 is a rewrite, not a rename. The utility names you already know mostly survived, so a `bg-rose-500` from a v3 codebase still works in v4. What changed is the ground under those names: the Preflight defaults, the CSS properties the transform utilities emit, the order stacked variants apply, and the syntax for arbitrary values. A migration is mostly an audit: walk every place you relied on a v3 default and decide whether the new default is what you want or whether you have to restate it.",
          "mental_model": "Tailwind v4 keeps the utility names that made Tailwind popular and rebuilds the engine underneath. The className strings you write are mostly unchanged; the CSS those strings compile to has shifted in ways that affect borders, rings, placeholders, buttons, dialogs, transforms, and the variant order. A migration that does not audit these defaults ships subtle visual regressions: a border that paints in `currentColor` instead of gray-200, a focus ring that is 1px instead of 3px, a placeholder that is solid instead of 60% opacity, a button cursor that is `default` instead of `pointer`.  You will meet this in real codebases in three places. First, a v3 project you inherit gets the v4 upgrade and the audit checklist is your first task. Second, a v4 design system you build has to choose whether to accept the new defaults or restate the old ones; the `@theme` block is the right place to set the project-wide preferences. Third, a v4 dependency you consume (a UI kit, a design-system package) may rely on `@theme inline` or `theme(static)`, and you have to know the difference to debug \"why is my variable empty in production?\" bugs.",
          "code_example": "/* v4 output */\n.border { border-color: currentColor; }\n.border-2 { border-color: currentColor; border-width: 2px; }",
          "pitfalls": "In v4 it paints `currentColor`. A card with `className=\"border p-4\"` whose parent is dark-mode-on will have a white border on a dark background. If you wanted gray-200 (the v3 default), write `border-gray-200`.  v4 ignores `bg-opacity-50`. Replace with `bg-red-500/50`.  v4 renamed them. `shrink-0` and `grow-1` are the v4 spellings. A v3 codebase migrating to v4 needs every `flex-shrink-*` and `flex-grow-*` swapped.  `overflow-ellipsis` is removed; `text-ellipsis` is the v4 spelling. A global find-and-replace handles most migrations.  `rounded-sm` in v4 is a smaller radius than v3. If your design used v3 `rounded-sm`, you want `rounded-xs` in v4. The same shift applies to `shadow-sm` → `shadow-xs` and `blur-sm` → `blur-xs`."
        },
