        "total_duration_seconds": 355,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "A component library is like a box of tested building parts. You assemble buttons, alerts, navigation, and forms instead of cutting every piece from raw CSS. The tradeoff is that the parts arrive with an API and a visual opinion.",
          "mental_model": "Component libraries shorten routine UI work and make interaction patterns consistent. Their props also become architectural boundaries: application state enters through typed props, events leave through callbacks, and theme values coordinate the screen. Knowing where the library stops prevents accessibility regressions, invalid prop values, and state hidden inside visual components.",
          "code_example": "bun add @mui/material @emotion/react @emotion/styled",
          "pitfalls": "Installing only `@mui/material` can produce:  Install `@emotion/react` and `@emotion/styled` with MUI's core package.  This is deliberately broken:  Use `variant=\"contained\"`, or augment MUI's variant types when your theme defines a real custom variant.  `<Checkbox indeterminate />` draws a mixed state, but the native input does not submit an `indeterminate` value. Store the domain state yourself and map it to `checked` and `indeterminate`."
        },
