        "id": "05-035",
        "number": 35,
        "title": "Material UI deep imports for bundle size",
        "local_chapter_file": "035-material-ui-deep-imports-for-bundle-size.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Mental model",
          "The deep-import rule",
          "Minimal example",
          "Enforce the rule with ESLint",
          "Predict the render",
          "Common mistakes"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 240,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 1 · **Prerequisites:** Chip, Component Libraries: Material UI  This chapter covers two things. First, the deep-import rule that keeps Material UI's dev server fast (barrel imports do not change the production bundle, but they do slow startup and rebuild). Second, a reference roundup of nine neighbouring MUI primitives you will reach for next to `Chip` and `Button`: `Divider`, `Fab`, `Link`, `Paper`, `Progress` indicators, `RadioGroup`, `Skeleton`, the `createTheme` / `ThemeProvider` pair, and the responsive viewport meta tag. The two exercises at the end combine most of these into one UI slice and then re-type it under strict TypeScript.",
          "mental_model": "Material UI's package layout is a directory of files with one default export per file, plus one barrel file (`index.js`) that re-exports them all. Your bundler can resolve either path:  In production, both paths ship the same tree-shaken result. In development, the barrel forces the dev server to parse every named export to learn which symbols exist before it can throw a `ReferenceError` when you mistype one. That parse takes a long time. The `@mui/icons-material` package is worse: it ships 2,100+ components, and the barrel parse takes long enough that you can watch it happen.",
          "code_example": "import Button from '@mui/material/Button';\nimport DeleteIcon from '@mui/icons-material/Delete';\n\nexport default function ConfirmDelete() {\n  return (\n    <Button\n      variant=\"contained\"\n      color=\"error\"\n      startIcon={<DeleteIcon />}\n      onClick={() => console.log('confirmed')}\n    >\n      Delete account\n    </Button>\n  );\n}",
          "pitfalls": "ESLint with the `no-restricted-imports` pattern from above reports:  Replace with `import Button from '@mui/material/Button';`. Add `@mui/icons-material/[IconName]` for icons.  VS Code by default adds barrel-import suggestions. Stop that for MUI with a project-scoped setting:  `@mui/icons-material` depends on `@mui/material`, which depends on Emotion. Installing the icon package without the core produces runtime errors when the first icon mounts:  The core trio + icons is the minimum. Add `@fontsource/roboto` if you want the default font."
        },
