        "total_duration_seconds": 689,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 1 · **Prerequisites:** Chip, Component Libraries: Material UI  This chapter is a reference roundup. The slug's namesake, **MUI Supported Components**, frames Material UI's scope: the library ships the practical building blocks of Material Design but does not promise every component or every guideline feature. Around that, the page documents **Supported Platforms** (which browsers, runtimes, and bundlers are first-class), the **System props to `sx`** codemod for the v6/v9 migration, the **TextareaAutosize** utility that auto-grows a `<textarea>`, the **Timeline** lab component for chronological content, the **Versioning Strategy** (semantic versioning with deprecation windows), the **Changelog Policy** (`CHANGELOG.md` for the current major, `CHANGELOG.old.md` for archives), the **Long-Term Support** policy (security and regression fixes for the predecessor major), and the four core building blocks the rest of the section leans on: **Paper** (surface + elevation), **Card** (paper with composable parts), **Alert** (transient feedback with severity), **Progress** (LinearProgress and CircularProgress), and **Radio** + **RadioGroup** (roving-tabindex single-select).",
          "mental_model": "Material UI's stance is explicit: it provides the practical building blocks developers need to build compelling user interfaces, omitting incidental or rarely-used components. The library does not aim to implement every Material Design component or every guideline feature. If a component exists in the Material Design specification but has no clear use in real apps, it does not ship in `@mui/material`. If you need it, you either build it yourself or reach for a community extension in `@mui/x` or a related project.  The practical effect of this scope:",
          "code_example": "bun add @mui/material @emotion/react @emotion/styled\nbun add @mui/icons-material",
          "pitfalls": "Direct DOM mutation or impure side effects during the render phase cause hydration mismatches, duplicate executions in Strict Mode, and stale closures. Always declare full dependency arrays in useEffect and never read state during render from outside the component tree."
        },
