        "total_duration_seconds": 492,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "A hardware shop sorts parts by job: switches control power, gauges display values, and fasteners join pieces. Material UI's catalog works the same way. Start with the job your interface must perform, then choose a component from the matching category.  **Difficulty:** 1 · **Prerequisites:** Pagination Query Parameters, Component Libraries: Material UI, Chip",
          "mental_model": "Real screens mix categories. A settings page may use `TextField`, `Select`, `Switch`, `Divider`, `Button`, and `Snackbar` in one component tree. Choosing by behavior prevents controls that look right but carry the wrong semantics. Controlled props keep application state visible, theme context keeps visual decisions consistent, and matching skeleton dimensions prevents layout shifts while data loads.",
          "code_example": "import Button from '@mui/material/Button';\nimport Chip from '@mui/material/Chip';\nimport Divider from '@mui/material/Divider';\nimport Stack from '@mui/material/Stack';\n\nexport default function Actions() {\n  return (\n    <Stack spacing={2} divider={<Divider flexItem />}>\n      <Chip label=\"Draft\" size=\"small\" />\n      <Button variant=\"contained\">Publish</Button>\n    </Stack>\n  );\n}",
          "pitfalls": "This is deliberately broken:  Use `variant=\"contained\"`, or add a typed theme variant before using a custom name.  A visual placeholder is not a label. An unlabeled `Select` may produce an accessibility audit such as:  Connect `InputLabel` and `Select` with `labelId`, or use `TextField` with `select` and `label`.  Inside a flex row, `<Divider orientation=\"vertical\" />` can collapse to an unexpected height because the separator does not automatically match the row. Add `flexItem`:"
        },
