              "mental_model": "A Material UI color object is a colour dictionary in the same shape as Material Design's design tokens. Each colour has a `main` shade, a `light` and `dark` companion, and a `contrastText` shade that picks readable text on top of `main`. The palette tokens you address in components (`primary`, `secondary`, `error`, `warning`, `info`, `success`) are slots on a `palette` object that each reference a colour object.  You can either hand-build a color object (good for full control) or import a prebuilt hue from `@mui/material/colors` and pass its `main` shade into `createTheme` (good for a quick branded seed). Components read the `palette.primary.main` token through React context, so the same colour shows up everywhere without prop drilling.",
              "code_example": "bun add @mui/material @emotion/react @emotion/styled\nbun add @mui/icons-material",
              "pitfalls": "The locale is not a `ThemeProvider` prop. The compiler does not always catch this because both accept a string-shaped prop. Pass the locale to `createTheme`:  If a `Snackbar` or `Tooltip` mounts at the same time, your backdrop sits behind them. Read `theme.zIndex.drawer + 1` (or whichever neighbour you need to be above) and let the theme own the layering scale.  The import works. The next minor release can rename or remove it. Add a comment at the call site so the next maintainer knows the surface is unstable and can plan a migration.  `component` swaps the root element; the `variant` styles then target a `<div>` instead of a `<button>`. The result looks the same visually but loses the button semantics (no form submission, no native click activation). Use `component` for router links and similar semantic swaps, not for visual overrides.  In dark mode, `Paper elevation={0}` is visually identical to `elevation={1}` because the dark-mode gradient at elevation `0` is the same as the page background. The fix is to test in dark mode and pick the elevation that produces visible separation, or use `variant=\"outlined\"` for a deterministic border."
