        "id": "05-043",
        "number": 43,
        "title": "Material UI theme provider",
        "local_chapter_file": "043-material-ui-theme-provider.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Mental model",
          "createTheme and ThemeProvider",
          "Theme scoping with THEME_ID",
          "theme.transitions.create and getAutoHeightDuration",
          "Themed components (default props, slot overrides, variants)",
          "Transfer List pattern"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 838,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 2 · **Prerequisites:** Material UI component catalog, Material UI Color System, Component Libraries: Material UI, Chip, Material UI deep imports for bundle size, Material UI MCP documentation server  This chapter is a reference roundup. The headline topic is the `ThemeProvider` and the `createTheme` factory, but the page also covers the surfaces and patterns you reach for when you wire a theme into a real app: scoping MUI alongside another styling system with `THEME_ID`, the `theme.transitions.create` and `getAutoHeightDuration` motion helpers, the theme's `components` section for default-props and slot-level overrides, the `TransferList` shuttle composition pattern, the five transition primitives (`Collapse`, `Fade`, `Grow`, `Slide`, `Zoom`), the migration paths from v0 to v1 and v3 to v4, the v5 component-level and style-level breaking changes, the v7 `enableCssLayer` and standardized slots update, the `theme.zIndex` scale, the minimal-bug-reproduction rule for filing MUI issues, the bundle-size minimization story, and the `Modal` primitive that `Dialog`, `Drawer`, `Menu`, and `Popover` all build on.",
          "mental_model": "The `ThemeProvider` is a React context provider whose value is a fully built `Theme` object. `createTheme(options)` walks the options, fills every unset token from a default, and returns a complete, deeply-typed `Theme`. The provider hands that theme down the tree, and every MUI component (and every `styled` / `sx` / `useTheme` call) reads it from context. Move the provider, change the theme; the visible app re-themes without a single prop-drilled colour.  When two styling systems coexist in the same tree (MUI plus Theme UI, MUI plus Chakra, MUI plus a custom design-token context), the providers must agree on which context key carries which theme. MUI exposes `THEME_ID` for exactly that negotiation: a string constant you put on a non-default context, so MUI's `styled`, `sx`, and `useTheme` look up the right value while other libraries keep using the default key.",
          "code_example": "bun add @mui/material @emotion/react @emotion/styled",
          "pitfalls": "`useTheme` from `@mui/material/styles` looks up the value under the `THEME_ID` constant, not under `myCustomKey`. The button will render with the default theme or throw depending on the MUI version. Use `[THEME_ID]: materialTheme`.  The property is `theme.transitions.duration.standard`. `theme.duration` is undefined. A rename of the namespace would be a breaking change, so the chain stays as is.  The button's icon slot is `startIcon` and `endIcon`, not `icon`. The wrong name compiles because the slot map is open-ended, but the override never applies. The fix is the slot name printed in the component's `slots` table.  A `Snackbar` at `zIndex.tooltip` or a `Dialog` at `zIndex.modal` can stack above the hand-picked number. Use `theme.zIndex.drawer + 1` (or whichever neighbour the backdrop needs to exceed) and let the theme own the scale.  Production tree-shaking still works, but the dev server parses the entire barrel before resolving the named exports. Deep imports keep dev fast. Enforce the rule with the `no-restricted-imports` ESLint pattern."
        },
