        "total_duration_seconds": 683,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 1-2 · **Prerequisites:** Component Libraries: Material UI, Material UI component catalog, Material UI Color System, Dialog, Popper  This chapter is a reference roundup built around the `Timeline` component, the chronological list surface that comes up in changelogs, order histories, audit logs, and activity feeds. Around the `Timeline` sits the larger MUI layout and theming toolkit a `Timeline` composes with: the `theme.breakpoints` API (the five screen-width keys and the `up` / `down` / `only` / `between` helpers) that lets the layout collapse from alternating to single-column on small screens, `Container` for horizontally centered page content, `Grid` (MUI 6 layout grid) for the responsive two-column variant of the `Timeline`, the `theme.components` overrides system that tunes every `TimelineDot` in the app at once, density props that tighten the `Timeline` on dense surfaces, `defaultProps` merging for class-name and style defaults, routing integration for the optional click-through timeline, palette tokens for the dot colour, `theme.spacing()` for the row gap, and `Stack` for the inner content column.",
          "mental_model": "A `Timeline` is a chronological list. Each entry is a `TimelineItem` whose visual structure is a `TimelineSeparator` (the line and the dot) sitting next to a `TimelineContent` (the title and body). The optional `TimelineOppositeContent` provides a second column used for dates or timestamps in alternating layouts.  The `Timeline` owns chrome (lines, dots, spacing). The `TimelineContent` is yours to fill: a typography node, a card, a button, a `Stack` of chips. The component is open enough to render anything inside the content slot but opinionated enough to keep the visual rhythm consistent.",
          "code_example": "bun add @mui/material @emotion/react @emotion/styled",
          "pitfalls": "Direct DOM mutation or impure side effects during the render phase cause hydration mismatches, duplicate executions in Strict Mode, and stale closures. Always declare full dependency arrays in useEffect and never read state during render from outside the component tree."
        },
