        "id": "05-052",
        "number": 52,
        "title": "Material UI with Tailwind CSS v4 (CSS layers) (and a roundup: `Menubar` from Base UI, `NumberField` from Base UI, JSS to Emotion migration, MUI API design, `Autocomplete`, v9 upgrade highlights, `ClassNameGenerator`, MUI composition, composition with `component` and `slots`, themed custom components, MUI CSS cascade layers, MUI CSS theme variables, MUI `Drawer` variants, MUI extensible themes)",
        "local_chapter_file": "052-material-ui-with-tailwind-css-v4-css-layers.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Mental model",
          "MUI + Tailwind v4 setup",
          "The MUI CSS layer theme options",
          "`Menubar` (Base UI composition recipe)",
          "`NumberField` (Base UI composition recipe)",
          "JSS to Emotion migration"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 535,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 2-3 · **Prerequisites:** Component Libraries: Material UI, Chip, Material UI component catalog, Material UI theme provider, MUI Timeline component  This chapter opens with the integration that lets Material UI and Tailwind v4 share the same DOM without `!important` wars: CSS cascade layers. MUI ships `experimental_extendTheme({ cssLayers: { ... } })` (and the corresponding `enableCssLayer` on the theme) to wrap every generated Emotion stylesheet in `@layer mui.global`, `@layer mui.components`, `@layer mui.theme`, `@layer mui.custom`, and `@layer mui.sx`; Tailwind v4 reads those layers and orders its own utilities last, so a `className=\"bg-red-500\"` always wins. Around the integration sits the rest of the MUI vocabulary the layered theme composes with: the documented `Menubar` recipe that composes Base UI's primitives with MUI components, the `NumberField` recipe that wires Base UI's `NumberField` to MUI form controls, the JSS to Emotion migration path for v4 to v5 codebases, the MUI API design conventions (low-level composition, consistent prop naming, root prop spreading, slot customisation, controlled state, root ref forwarding), the `Autocomplete` combobox (props like `options`, `value`/`onChange`, `inputValue`/`onInputChange`, `getOptionLabel`, `isOptionEqualToValue`, `multiple`, `freeSolo`, `groupBy`, `renderGroup`, `renderInput`, `renderOption`, `filterOptions`, `createFilterOptions`, plus the headless `useAutocomplete`), the v9 upgrade highlights (Enter/Space clicks now bubble as `MouseEvent`, the `nativeButton` prop for `<div>`-based custom buttons, the `AutocompleteValueOrFreeSoloValueMapping` typing for `freeSolo`, `Backdrop` no longer adding `aria-hidden` by default), the `unstable_ClassNameGenerator` build-time hook for rewriting the MUI class-name prefix, the composition guide (forwarded props, `muiName` preservation, merged slot props, polymorphic roots, forwarded refs), the composition patterns that use the `component` and `slots`/`slotProps` props plus `mergeSlotProps`, the themed custom components recipe (`styled(..., { name: 'MuiXxx', slot: 'root' })` so the theme can override the component via `components.MuiXxx.styleOverrides` and expose defaults via `defaultProps`/`useThemeProps`), the `cssVariables` mode that emits theme tokens as CSS custom properties (`--mui-palette-primary-main`, `theme.vars`, `colorSchemeSelector`, `useColorScheme`, `InitColorSchemeScript`), the `Drawer` variants (`temporary`, `persistent`, `mini`, `permanent`, `swipeable`), and the extensible-theme pattern that splits tokens and component defaults across separate exports so consuming apps can extend with object spread and preserve component variants with array syntax.",
          "mental_model": "CSS cascade layers (`@layer a, b, c;`) define a strict order of precedence across the whole stylesheet. A rule inside `@layer a` always loses to a rule in `@layer b`, regardless of selector specificity. Tailwind v4 emits every utility class inside `@layer utilities;` so that any user-land `@layer theme` or `@layer components` overrides are guaranteed to lose to a Tailwind utility. The MUI integration simply wraps every Emotion-generated stylesheet inside `@layer mui.*` so it sits inside the cascade order too. With both sides agreeing on the layer order, you never need `!important`.  L1[\"@layer theme, base\"]:::base   L2[\"@layer mui.global\"]:::mui   L3[\"@layer mui.components\"]:::mui   L4[\"@layer mui.theme\"]:::mui   L5[\"@layer mui.custom\"]:::mui   L6[\"@layer mui.sx\"]:::mui   L7[\"@layer components\"]:::base   L8[\"@layer utilities\"]:::tw",
          "code_example": "bun add @mui/material @emotion/react @emotion/styled",
          "pitfalls": "// Right: inject the initial color scheme into the document before React hydrates <html>   <head>     <InitColorSchemeScript attribute=\"class\" />   </head>   <body>     <ThemeProvider theme={theme}>{children}</ThemeProvider>   </body> </html> ```  /* Right: declare the layer order at the top of the entry stylesheet */ @layer theme, base, mui, components, utilities;  @import \"tailwindcss\"; ```  // Right: also declare the order in CSS // @layer theme, base, mui, components, utilities; ```  // Right: pick one; component for simple element swaps, slots for slot-level swaps <Button component={RouterLink} to=\"/save\">Save</Button> // or <Button slots={{ root: MyRoot }}>Save</Button> ```"
        },
