              "mental_model": "A shared brand package can serve several products while each product chooses its palette, navigation, and component behavior. Explicit merging makes that boundary visible in code. Ordered style arrays prevent a release from silently removing focus, hover, or disabled-state rules. Avoiding an eager deep merge also keeps theme construction out of the render path and removes avoidable startup work.  The component roundup gives the merged theme real consumers: a `Drawer` or `Menu` for navigation, a `Dialog` or `Backdrop` for attention, and controlled input components for preferences. Each consumer reads the final theme from `ThemeProvider`, so a change to brand tokens reaches the whole tree without prop drilling.",
              "code_example": "import { createTheme } from '@mui/material/styles';\nimport type { ThemeOptions } from '@mui/material/styles';\n\nconst brand: ThemeOptions = {\n  palette: { primary: { main: '#111827' } },\n  shape: { borderRadius: 6 },\n};\n\nconst appTheme = createTheme({\n  ...brand,\n  palette: {\n    ...brand.palette,\n    primary: { ...brand.palette?.primary, main: '#2563eb' },\n  },\n});",
              "pitfalls": "The code compiles, but the brand's `minWidth` and pseudo-class rules vanish. Spread the branded `MuiButton` entry and put the two roots in an array.  Spread `brand.palette.primary` before changing `main`, and repeat that pattern for nested groups you customize.  If you render `<Switch checked={enabled} />` without an `onChange` that updates `enabled`, the control looks stuck. React does not change the prop for you. Keep the source of truth in state.  `ToggleButtonGroup` calls its handler with `null` after the selected button is clicked again. Passing that value into a state typed as `View` produces `Type 'null' is not assignable to type 'View'`. Check `next !== null` before updating."
