        "id": "05-056",
        "number": 56,
        "title": "MUI Tabs and Tab components",
        "local_chapter_file": "056-mui-tabs-and-tab-components.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "A settings binder",
          "The concept: a coordinated selection value",
          "Minimal example",
          "Predict the render",
          "Form-control reference",
          "Theme and migration reference"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 824,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Prerequisites:** useActionState + React 19 Form Actions, useContext, Forms with React Hook Form, Component Libraries: Material UI, Material UI component catalog, MUI Table Composition, MUI Timeline component, Material UI MCP documentation server, Chip  This is a reference-heavy chapter. It starts with tabs, then collects MUI form controls and styling APIs that use the same two ideas: controlled values and context shared by a parent component.",
          "mental_model": "You meet these components in settings screens, onboarding flows, search filters, review forms, and admin tools. A shared controlled-value model makes their behavior predictable, while the MUI parent handles focus and keyboard details that are easy to omit in hand-built widgets.  The context hooks and slot APIs also create supported customization boundaries. They prevent code from reaching into private markup or copying internal state. Migration work then becomes a change of package, styling engine, or extraction model without rewriting the form's domain state.",
          "code_example": "import { useState } from \"react\";\nimport type { SyntheticEvent } from \"react\";\nimport Tab from \"@mui/material/Tab\";\nimport Tabs from \"@mui/material/Tabs\";\n\nexport function AccountTabs() {\n  const [active, setActive] = useState<\"profile\" | \"security\">(\"profile\");\n\n  const selectTab = (_event: SyntheticEvent, next: string): void => {\n    if (next === \"profile\" || next === \"security\") {\n      setActive(next);\n    }\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      <Tabs value={active} onChange={selectTab} aria-label=\"Account sections\">\n        <Tab\n          value=\"profile\"\n          label=\"Profile\"\n          id=\"account-tab-profile\"\n          aria-controls=\"account-panel-profile\"\n        />\n        <Tab\n          value=\"security\"\n          label=\"Security\"\n          id=\"account-tab-security\"\n          aria-controls=\"account-panel-security\"\n        />\n      </Tabs>\n      <div\n        role=\"tabpanel\"\n        id={`account-panel-${active}`}\n        aria-labelledby={`account-tab-${active}`}\n      >\n        {active === \"profile\" ? \"Edit your name\" : \"Change your password\"}\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  );\n}",
          "pitfalls": "A `Tab` with `aria-controls=\"security-panel\"` and a panel with `id=\"profile-panel\"` compiles and does not log a runtime error. The accessibility tree loses the relationship. Derive both IDs from the same tab value, or use the Lab components to wire the relationship.  This deliberately broken TypeScript drops the clear state:  Use `useState<number | null>(3)` or reject `null` deliberately when the product requires a rating.  An `Autocomplete` value loaded from one request may have the same fields as an option loaded from another request, but it is a different object reference. The component cannot match it by identity and may log:  Add `isOptionEqualToValue={(option, value) => option.id === value.id}`. Keep IDs stable and unique."
        },
