        "id": "05-057",
        "number": 57,
        "title": "Pigment CSS (zero-runtime CSS-in-JS) (and a roundup: the `css` / `globalCss` / `keyframes` / `styled` APIs, `variants` vs runtime callbacks, `withPigment` plugins for Vite and Next.js, `extendTheme` and `vars`, the `sx` prop, polymorphic MUI wrappers, MUI v7 and v9 upgrade steps, `prettier-plugin-tailwindcss` sorting and collapse, `@tailwindui/react` `<Transition>`, shadcn UI's copy-paste model, CSS Modules, component extraction, the component tree, `createRoot`, and one-way data flow)",
        "local_chapter_file": "057-pigment-css-zero-runtime-css-in-js.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Mental model",
          "Install and configure Pigment CSS",
          "The `css` API",
          "The `globalCss` API",
          "The `keyframes` API",
          "The `styled` API"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 491,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "**Difficulty:** 1-4 · **Prerequisites:** Chip, Material UI Color System, MUI Table Composition, Components and Props, Domain-first data model planning, React Canary Release Channel  This chapter opens with **Pigment CSS**, MUI's zero-runtime CSS-in-JS engine: it pre-compiles styles at build time, emits real CSS, and is the path forward if you want a `styled`-like authoring experience while staying React Server Components compatible. Around that headline sits the rest of the styling vocabulary that the longer Styling section has been pulling together. The Pigment CSS APIs (`css`, `globalCss`, `keyframes`, `styled`, `variants`, runtime callbacks) are covered in depth, the Vite and Next.js plugins are explained as the entry points, the `extendTheme` / `vars` pattern is shown as the bridge between the build-time theme and runtime CSS variables, and the `sx` prop is described as the inline-style escape hatch that compiles down to `className` + `style` at build time. The roundup that follows covers the older Material UI `TypographyProps`-based polymorphic wrapper pattern, the v7 and v9 upgrade steps (package exports, slot standardisation, CSS layers, Grid renaming, accessibility improvements), the two halves of `prettier-plugin-tailwindcss` (fixed-order sorting and the v1.7+ collapse pass for whitespace and duplicates), the early `@tailwindui/react` `<Transition>` primitive, the shadcn UI copy-paste model that re-shapes how a team owns its component library, CSS Modules as a third styling option next to Pigment and Tailwind, the Wieruch component-tree / `createRoot` / one-way data flow foundations, and the extraction heuristic that keeps a component understandable.",
          "mental_model": "A \"CSS-in-JS\" library writes a stylesheet that ships JavaScript to the browser, then inserts rules into the DOM on render. Emotion (the engine behind `@mui/material`) does this. The runtime cost is real: every render has to diff the styles, and the styles themselves have to live in the bundle. Pigment CSS inverts the relationship. The `css` and `styled` calls are still TypeScript, but a build plugin (Vite or Next.js) reads the source, emits a real CSS file, and rewrites the call sites so the runtime only does `className` lookup. The browser sees a `<link rel=\"stylesheet\">` and a class string, nothing more.  The build-time flavour has three consequences worth naming up front:",
          "code_example": "bun add @mui/material @emotion/react @emotion/styled\nbun add @pigment-css/react\nbun add --save-dev @pigment-css/vite-plugin",
          "pitfalls": "**1. Forgetting the stylesheet import.** The Pigment CSS build plugin writes the CSS file, but the bundler does not know to serve it. The browser shows a flash of unstyled content followed by the layout, because the CSS is loaded but its `<link>` is in the wrong place. Fix:  createRoot(document.getElementById(\"root\")!).render(<App />); ```  **2. Using `useTheme` from `@mui/material/styles` instead of `@mui/material-pigment-css`.** The first is the Emotion runtime hook and returns the resolved theme object; the second is the Pigment CSS hook and returns the `theme.vars` proxy. The types are similar but the values differ. The Emotion hook works in Pigment projects only because the Emotion engine is still installed; the values are the resolved objects, not the CSS variables. The Pigment hook is the one that matches the generated CSS.  // Right import { useTheme } from \"@mui/material-pigment-css\"; ```  **3. Using a callback for an enumerable prop.** A callback that reads `props.variant` and switches on a known list of strings is slower and less obvious than the `variants` array:"
        },
