        "id": "05-059",
        "number": 59,
        "title": "Themed styled-components with TypeScript",
        "local_chapter_file": "059-themed-styled-components-with-typescript.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The tagged template, briefly",
          "Typing the theme",
          "`ThemeProvider` injects the value through context",
          "`createGlobalStyle` reuses the same theme",
          "Transient `$` props keep style-only data off the DOM",
          "Linting the CSS inside the templates"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 976,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "A styled-component is a React component whose styles live in a tagged template literal right next to the component. You write CSS in the template, and the library returns a real component you render like any other:  const Button = styled.button`   padding: 8px 16px;   border-radius: 6px;   border: none;   background: #0a84ff;   color: white; `;",
          "mental_model": "A themed component library is two systems working together. The theme plus typed templates give you styles that read from one design source and cannot silently misspell a token. The state architecture (minimal state, a clear owner, lifting, and the controlled-versus- uncontrolled choice) decides what those styles react to and keeps the values that drive them in exactly one place. Get the theme typed and the ownership right, and a tab strip, a paginator, a stepper, and a form all end up with the same predictable shape: value down, change up, styles derived from the theme.",
          "code_example": "import styled from \"styled-components\";\n\nconst Button = styled.button`\n  padding: 8px 16px;\n  border-radius: 6px;\n  border: none;\n  background: #0a84ff;\n  color: white;\n`;\n\n// <Button>Save</Button>  ->  a <button> with those styles attached",
          "pitfalls": "Without `ThemeProps<AppTheme>` (or a `DefaultTheme` augmentation), `props.theme` widens to `any` and `colours` (British misspelling) sails through the compiler. Type the theme once and the typo becomes an error at the call site.  Rename it `$filled`. The transient prefix keeps the prop out of the rendered attributes while still feeding the interpolation.  Keeping a `filteredList` in `useState` and updating it in an effect gives you two sources of truth that drift. Compute the filtered list from the source list and the query during render. State holds only the inputs; the output is derived.  If two siblings each keep their own copy of the \"same\" value, they will disagree the moment one updates without the other. Lift the value into their common parent and pass it down. One owner, one value.  Controlled state on a field that no other part of the UI reads adds a render per keystroke for no benefit. If only the final value matters, leave the field uncontrolled (a `defaultValue`, or React Hook Form's `register`) and read the value when you submit."
        },
