        "id": "06-001",
        "number": 1,
        "title": "Axios cross-runtime features",
        "local_chapter_file": "001-axios-cross-runtime-features.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The concept",
          "Your first request",
          "One configured client: `axios.create`",
          "Typing requests",
          "Narrowing errors",
          "Running requests in parallel"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 1137,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "A travel adapter is a small block with one socket on the front and a different plug on the back. Your laptop charger never learns about British, European, or Japanese outlets. It plugs into the same socket every time, and the block deals with the wall.  Axios is that block for HTTP. Your code calls `axios.get(...)` and gets a promise back. Underneath, the library picks the plug that fits the runtime it woke up in: `XMLHttpRequest` in a browser, Node's `http` module on a server, or `fetch` when you ask for it.",
          "mental_model": "The moment your app runs in two places, this stops being a style question. A component fetches in the browser; the same endpoint gets called during server rendering, in a route handler, or in a test. One client with one response shape means one set of error paths to reason about instead of a browser branch and a server branch.  The pieces here are also the substrate for the rest of this section. TanStack Query needs a query function that returns a promise and honors an `AbortSignal`; a wrapped Axios call is exactly that. React's cleanup story for in-flight requests is `AbortController`. The single-client pattern from `axios.create` is where interceptors, auth headers, and XSRF configuration eventually live.",
          "code_example": "// Three ways to consume the same promise.\naxios.get(\"/api/users\").then((response) => console.log(response.data));\n\nconst response = await axios.get(\"/api/users\"); // inside an async function\n\naxios\n  .get(\"/api/users\")\n  .then((response) => console.log(response.data))\n  .catch((error) => console.error(error.message))\n  .finally(() => console.log(\"done\"));",
          "pitfalls": "**Reading fields off the response instead of `data`.** The `fetch` habit of `response.json()` and `response.ok` does not carry over. Axios has already parsed the body.  There is no second step to await. Axios already ran the response transform and parsed the body before the promise settled. The same error shape hits `response.ok`:  Fix: `const post = response.data;` and check `response.status` when you need the code.  **Touching a caught error before narrowing it.**  Fix: guard with `axios.isAxiosError(error)` first, and rethrow whatever is not an Axios error."
        },
