        "id": "06-007",
        "number": 7,
        "title": "Queries and query result states",
        "local_chapter_file": "007-queries-and-query-result-states.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Mental model: a vending machine with a clipboard",
          "Server state, what TanStack Query is for",
          "`QueryClientProvider` setup",
          "`useQuery` and the result object",
          "The `queryKey`",
          "The `queryFn` and `AbortSignal`"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 1120,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Imagine a vending machine. You walk up, press a button (the `queryKey`), and the machine either gives you a snack (the `data`) or a red error light (the `error`). Either way, the machine also flips one of two small flags: `isFetching` (the coil is humming, a request is in flight right now) and `isPending` / `isError` / `isSuccess` (the most recent attempt settled into this state).  The interesting part is the clipboard the machine keeps behind the counter. When you press the same button a second time, the machine first checks the clipboard. If the entry is fresh enough, you get the snack immediately and the coil never hums. If the entry is stale, you get the snack from the clipboard and the machine kicks off a quiet refill in the background. A second customer pressing the same button in the same instant gets the same in-flight request, not a duplicate. The clipboard is the cache; the button is the `queryKey`; the refill is background refetching; the dedup is request deduplication.",
          "mental_model": "Imagine a vending machine. You walk up, press a button (the `queryKey`), and the machine either gives you a snack (the `data`) or a red error light (the `error`). Either way, the machine also flips one of two small flags: `isFetching` (the coil is humming, a request is in flight right now) and `isPending` / `isError` / `isSuccess` (the most recent attempt settled into this state).  The interesting part is the clipboard the machine keeps behind the counter. When you press the same button a second time, the machine first checks the clipboard. If the entry is fresh enough, you get the snack immediately and the coil never hums. If the entry is stale, you get the snack from the clipboard and the machine kicks off a quiet refill in the background. A second customer pressing the same button in the same instant gets the same in-flight request, not a duplicate. The clipboard is the cache; the button is the `queryKey`; the refill is background refetching; the dedup is request deduplication.",
          "code_example": "import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from \"@tanstack/react-query\";\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient({\n  defaultOptions: {\n    queries: {\n      staleTime: 60_000,\n      retry: 1,\n    },\n  },\n});\n\nexport function App({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {\n  return (\n    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n      {children}\n    </QueryClientProvider>\n  );\n}",
          "pitfalls": "**Treating `isPending` as the only loading flag.** A query can have cached data and still be fetching in the background. Showing a spinner whenever `isFetching` is true defeats the point of the cache. Show a spinner on `isPending`; show a \"refreshing\" hint on `isFetching` only when the cached data is already on screen.  **Reading `id` from a closure but not putting it in `queryKey`.** The cache entry is keyed by the array, not by what the `queryFn` closes over. A `queryKey` of `[\"post\"]` with a `queryFn` that reads `id` from props will keep returning the post for the first `id` you mounted with.  **Forgetting `signal` in the `queryFn`.** A `queryFn` that calls `fetch(url)` without forwarding the signal still works, but unmount mid-request does not cancel the network call. The response lands in a cache entry with zero observers, the cache holds it until `gcTime` elapses, and the request kept a TCP socket open the whole time.  **Treating `retry: 3` as a universal default.** A 401, a 404, and a 500 are not the same. A 401 might succeed on retry after a token refresh. A 404 will fail forever. A 500 might succeed on retry. Use the function form of `retry` to skip retries that cannot succeed.  **Wrapping the whole app in `<QueryClientProvider>` but creating a new `QueryClient` on every render.** `new QueryClient()` in a component body throws away the cache on every re-render of the parent. Create the client once (module scope or `useState`)."
        },
