        "id": "06-013",
        "number": 13,
        "title": "`queryOptions`",
        "local_chapter_file": "013-queryoptions.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "The `queryOptions` helper",
          "Options the factory accepts",
          "Sharing options across consumers",
          "`queryClient` methods that share the helper",
          "Targeted invalidation patterns",
          "TypeScript inference and the `Register` interface"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 645,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Difficulty: 2 · Prerequisites: `mutate` (`void`) vs `mutateAsync` (`Promise`) function signatures, Placeholder query data, Server State with TanStack Query, MUI Timeline component, Generic fetch wrapper module  A `useQuery` call duplicates the same three arguments across the component that reads the data, the route loader that prefetches it, the SSR dehydration that ships it to the client, and the devtools entry that inspects it: the `queryKey`, the `queryFn`, and a handful of per-query options (`staleTime`, `retry`, `select`). If any of those drift, the cache treats the entries as different and the app silently fetches twice.",
          "mental_model": "The `queryOptions` helper is the seam between \"the cache contract for this resource\" and \"every consumer that reads or writes that cache.\" Without it, the contract lives in five places (a hook, a prefetch, three call sites that invalidate) and drift is inevitable. With it, the contract lives in one file and every consumer gets typed access for free.  The pattern shows up in real codebases in three places:",
          "code_example": "import { queryOptions } from \"@tanstack/react-query\";\n\ninterface Post {\n  readonly id: number;\n  readonly title: string;\n}\n\nasync function fetchPost(id: number, signal: AbortSignal): Promise<Post> {\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/posts/${id}`, { signal });\n  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);\n  return res.json() as Promise<Post>;\n}\n\nexport function postQueryOptions(id: number) {\n  return queryOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"post\", id] as const,\n    queryFn: ({ signal }) => fetchPost(id, signal),\n    staleTime: 60_000,\n  });\n}",
          "pitfalls": "- **Forgetting `as const` on the `queryKey`.** Without it, the `queryKey` type widens to `string[]` and the brand on `queryOptions` is weaker. Most editors will still infer the right shape, but the brand's `TData` resolution goes through `unknown[]` and the type goes fuzzy at `getQueryData` call sites. - **Re-defining the options at each call site.** The single biggest reason `queryOptions` exists is to remove duplication. If a component hand-writes `{ queryKey: [\"post\", id], queryFn: ({ signal }) => fetchPost(id, signal) }` next to `useQuery`, the helper is not paying its rent. - **Calling `getQueryData` with a raw key and a generic.** `queryClient.getQueryData<Post>([\"post\", 1])` is what you do when there is no helper. Once the helper exists, `getQueryData(postQueryOptions(1))` is the typed path; the raw-key-with-generic form is a typo magnet. - **Setting `staleTime: Infinity` and then forgetting to invalidate.** `Infinity` keeps data fresh until manual invalidation. If a mutation does not invalidate the right key, the UI shows a stale value forever. The fix is either a shorter `staleTime` or an explicit `invalidateQueries` in the mutation's `onSuccess`. - **Polling with `refetchInterval` but no background pause.** A 5s poll on a tab the user is not looking at can hammer a small API. The fix is `refetchIntervalInBackground: false`. - **Setting `subscribed: false` on a hook that should re-render.** `subscribed: false` detaches the observer from the cache, so the component will not re-render when the cached data updates. It is the right choice for one-shot reads and wrong for every other case. - **Spreading the result of `useQuery`.** `const { data, ...rest } = useQuery(...)` subscribes the component to every tracked property. Pull only what the render reads (`data`, `isPending`, `isError`, `error`, `isPlaceholderData`, `isFetching`, `refetch`). The `@tanstack/eslint-plugin-query` `no-rest-destructuring` rule catches this."
        },
