        "id": "06-018",
        "number": 18,
        "title": "Per-query persistence and advanced TanStack Query patterns",
        "local_chapter_file": "018-per-query-experimental-persister.md",
        "key_concepts": [
          "Two persistence models",
          "Minimal per-query setup",
          "`PersistQueryClientProvider`",
          "A short `Promise.all` primer",
          "Waterfalls and imperative infinite queries",
          "React Server Components as a prefetch phase"
        ],
        "total_duration_seconds": 812,
        "summary_notes": {
          "definition": "Difficulty: 2–4 · Prerequisites: Synchronous storage persister, Server State with TanStack Query, Queries and query result states, `skipToken`  Imagine a library with two storage systems. The reading room holds every book currently in use. An archive holds books between visits. You could copy the entire reading room into the archive whenever one book changes, or file each book under its own catalogue number and retrieve it only when someone asks for it.",
          "mental_model": "Persistence makes repeat visits fast and supports offline-first screens, but stale private data and mismatched cache keys are harder to notice than a failed request. A clear policy for identity, freshness, storage expiry, and restoration prevents cross-user data leaks and surprise refetches. Concurrent prefetching also removes latency that nested component fetches add one request at a time.",
          "code_example": "import { useQuery } from \"@tanstack/react-query\";\nimport { experimental_createQueryPersister } from \"@tanstack/react-query-persist-client\";\n\ninterface Settings {\n  readonly theme: \"light\" | \"dark\";\n}\n\nconst settingsPersister = experimental_createQueryPersister({\n  storage: window.localStorage,\n  maxAge: 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000,\n  prefix: \"course\",\n});\n\nasync function getSettings(): Promise<Settings> {\n  const response = await fetch(\"/api/settings\");\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n  return response.json() as Promise<Settings>;\n}\n\nexport function SettingsPanel() {\n  const query = useQuery({\n    queryKey: [\"settings\"] as const,\n    queryFn: getSettings,\n    staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000,\n    persister: settingsPersister.persisterFn,\n  });\n\n  if (query.isPending) return <p>Loading settings...</p>;\n  if (query.isError) return <p role=\"alert\">{query.error.message}</p>;\n  return <p>Theme: {query.data.theme}</p>;\n}",
          "pitfalls": "`@tanstack/eslint-plugin-query` supplies recommended rules for React Query code. Its exhaustive-key rule reports a query function variable that is absent from `queryKey`. The stable-client rule catches a `QueryClient` recreated during each render. Other rules cover unstable dependencies, infinite-query property order, and mutation callback usage.  If the function requests `/api/projects?owner=${owner}`, then `owner` belongs in `queryKey`:  The key is both a cache address and a dependency list. Leaving `owner` out can show one user's cached projects after the prop changes."
        },
